{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W6hGQUzYFqhr6QH9rYzbC7","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, here's what was available:\n\n## Review Criteria for Regular Papers\n\nPapers are evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical.\" Assessment considers \"objective establishment\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\nThe conference welcomes submissions combining experimental and theoretical work related to robot learning, positioning itself as \"a selective top-tier conference on robotic learning.\"\n\n## Reviewer Qualifications\n\nReviewers should demonstrate expertise through publications. The guidelines specify reviewers ideally have \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals (e.g. CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL) and core machine learning venues (e.g. NeurIPS, ICML, AIStats, JMLR, PAMI).\"\n\n## Notable Gaps\n\n**The provided content does not include:**\n- Numerical rating scales or scoring rubrics\n- Specific review form fields/templates\n- Acceptance percentage thresholds\n- Detailed scoring descriptions\n- Quantitative acceptance criteria\n\nThe document focuses on desk rejection criteria and reviewer qualifications rather than comprehensive review guidelines or rating systems."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"9385aca7-bc07-4658-8dfc-93242b05a4aa","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:12:42.646Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":134054,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, here's what was available:\n\n## Review Criteria for Regular Papers\n\nPapers are evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical.\" Assessment considers \"objective establishment\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\nThe conference welcomes submissions combining experimental and theoretical work related to robot learning, positioning itself as \"a selective top-tier conference on robotic learning.\"\n\n## Reviewer Qualifications\n\nReviewers should demonstrate expertise through publications. The guidelines specify reviewers ideally have \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals (e.g. CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL) and core machine learning venues (e.g. NeurIPS, ICML, AIStats, JMLR, PAMI).\"\n\n## Notable Gaps\n\n**The provided content does not include:**\n- Numerical rating scales or scoring rubrics\n- Specific review form fields/templates\n- Acceptance percentage thresholds\n- Detailed scoring descriptions\n- Quantitative acceptance criteria\n\nThe document focuses on desk rejection criteria and reviewer qualifications rather than comprehensive review guidelines or rating systems.","durationMs":3686,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019QcUsGFAsCnRBhexR1Ya6S","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Review Writing Guidelines\n\nThe review process evaluates papers across six dimensions:\n\n**Originality:** Assesses novelty of problems/methods and whether work represents a \"novel combination of well-known techniques.\" Reviewers should clarify how the submission differs from prior contributions.\n\n**Quality:** Examines technical soundness and whether \"claims well supported (e.g., by theoretical analysis or experimental results).\" Reviewers assess methodological appropriateness and completeness.\n\n**Clarity:** Evaluates whether the paper is \"clearly written\" and \"well organized,\" with sufficient detail for expert reproducibility.\n\n**Significance:** Determines if results advance the field and whether \"others (researchers or practitioners) likely to use the ideas or build on them.\"\n\n**Relevance:** Confirms the work addresses \"robot learning\" and applies \"learning-based method, or a learned model.\" Papers should demonstrate evaluation in simulation, real robots, or both.\n\n**Limitations:** Reviewers should appreciate honestly reported limitations while extensively documenting unreported shortcomings.\n\n## Key Guidance\n\nReviewers should be \"constructive and concrete; courteous and respectful.\" The guidelines emphasize avoiding common mistakes such as:\n- Rejecting work without citing evidence of prior similar research\n- Requiring code/datasets, theorems, or real-robot demonstrations as mandatory criteria\n- Equating SOTA performance with acceptance quality\n\n**Note:** The document does not specify a numeric rating scale (1-10) or explicit acceptance criteria thresholds."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"9398b519-4994-4950-869f-b40e1ed35bed","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:12:45.496Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Review Writing Guidelines\n\nThe review process evaluates papers across six dimensions:\n\n**Originality:** Assesses novelty of problems/methods and whether work represents a \"novel combination of well-known techniques.\" Reviewers should clarify how the submission differs from prior contributions.\n\n**Quality:** Examines technical soundness and whether \"claims well supported (e.g., by theoretical analysis or experimental results).\" Reviewers assess methodological appropriateness and completeness.\n\n**Clarity:** Evaluates whether the paper is \"clearly written\" and \"well organized,\" with sufficient detail for expert reproducibility.\n\n**Significance:** Determines if results advance the field and whether \"others (researchers or practitioners) likely to use the ideas or build on them.\"\n\n**Relevance:** Confirms the work addresses \"robot learning\" and applies \"learning-based method, or a learned model.\" Papers should demonstrate evaluation in simulation, real robots, or both.\n\n**Limitations:** Reviewers should appreciate honestly reported limitations while extensively documenting unreported shortcomings.\n\n## Key Guidance\n\nReviewers should be \"constructive and concrete; courteous and respectful.\" The guidelines emphasize avoiding common mistakes such as:\n- Rejecting work without citing evidence of prior similar research\n- Requiring code/datasets, theorems, or real-robot demonstrations as mandatory criteria\n- Equating SOTA performance with acceptance quality\n\n**Note:** The document does not specify a numeric rating scale (1-10) or explicit acceptance criteria thresholds.","durationMs":5791,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
