{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ULfeDxbQQXEFmA7kz3ibxt","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2021 Review Rating Scale and Rubric\n\nBased on the provided page content, **no rating scale, scoring rubric, numerical scores, or review form fields are specified**.\n\nThe document focuses exclusively on the review timeline and procedural requirements for reviewers. It outlines key phases:\n\n- Reviewer bidding and assignment (June 19-30)\n- Initial review period (July 1-23)\n- Quality checks and emergency reviews (July 24-Aug 7)\n- Author discussion period (Aug 16-30)\n- Final decisions (Aug 31-Sep 6)\n\nThe instructions emphasize that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement\" during discussion periods, but the actual evaluation criteria and scoring system are not detailed in this particular page.\n\nTo find the complete review rubric and rating scale, you would need to consult additional CoRL 2021 documentation, likely available through OpenReview or supplementary reviewer guidelines sent directly to reviewers."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"05f42806-1185-4fee-ae30-05f8ed330154","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:13:14.647Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":124975,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2021 Review Rating Scale and Rubric\n\nBased on the provided page content, **no rating scale, scoring rubric, numerical scores, or review form fields are specified**.\n\nThe document focuses exclusively on the review timeline and procedural requirements for reviewers. It outlines key phases:\n\n- Reviewer bidding and assignment (June 19-30)\n- Initial review period (July 1-23)\n- Quality checks and emergency reviews (July 24-Aug 7)\n- Author discussion period (Aug 16-30)\n- Final decisions (Aug 31-Sep 6)\n\nThe instructions emphasize that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement\" during discussion periods, but the actual evaluation criteria and scoring system are not detailed in this particular page.\n\nTo find the complete review rubric and rating scale, you would need to consult additional CoRL 2021 documentation, likely available through OpenReview or supplementary reviewer guidelines sent directly to reviewers.","durationMs":3675,"url":"https://2021.corl.org/reviewer-information/instructions-for-reviewers"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014GzFoQ5YESguNJFzUVn2WY","type":"tool_result","content":"# CORL 2025 Statistics Summary\n\nBased on the webpage content, here's what can be extracted about CORL 2025:\n\n**Acceptance Rate:**\nThe conference received 263 total submissions with 221 accepted papers (84.03% acceptance rate) and 42 oral presentations (15.97%).\n\n**Score Information:**\nUnfortunately, detailed score distributions and specific rating scales for CORL 2025 are not provided on this page. The table shows \"PagesLoading...\" for CORL 2025 metrics, indicating the data hasn't fully loaded.\n\n**Comparison with Previous Years:**\nFor context, CORL 2024 had a 39.34% acceptance rate from 671 submissions with scores on a 1.70-4.00 scale (average: 3.01). CORL 2023 used a 4.70-10.00 scale with a 39.96% acceptance rate.\n\n**Data Availability:**\nThe page indicates reviewers can submit \"rating,\" \"confidence,\" and \"replies\" metrics through OpenReview, but complete statistics for CORL 2025 appear still to be loading on the platform."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"d5ac0a8a-b8d8-4822-8d05-f1f614a0780f","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:13:16.494Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":219800,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CORL 2025 Statistics Summary\n\nBased on the webpage content, here's what can be extracted about CORL 2025:\n\n**Acceptance Rate:**\nThe conference received 263 total submissions with 221 accepted papers (84.03% acceptance rate) and 42 oral presentations (15.97%).\n\n**Score Information:**\nUnfortunately, detailed score distributions and specific rating scales for CORL 2025 are not provided on this page. The table shows \"PagesLoading...\" for CORL 2025 metrics, indicating the data hasn't fully loaded.\n\n**Comparison with Previous Years:**\nFor context, CORL 2024 had a 39.34% acceptance rate from 671 submissions with scores on a 1.70-4.00 scale (average: 3.01). CORL 2023 used a 4.70-10.00 scale with a 39.96% acceptance rate.\n\n**Data Availability:**\nThe page indicates reviewers can submit \"rating,\" \"confidence,\" and \"replies\" metrics through OpenReview, but complete statistics for CORL 2025 appear still to be loading on the platform.","durationMs":4501,"url":"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/corl-statistics/corl-2025-statistics/"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AWwHS3tNxV32dnkKcTG9S1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have some useful information:\n- CoRL 2024 used a 1-4 scale (average 3.01, range 1.70-4.00 for accepted papers)\n- CoRL 2023 used a 1-10 scale (range 4.70-10.00 for accepted)\n- CoRL acceptance rate ~30-40%\n\nActually wait, the 2025 data seems off (84% acceptance for 263 submissions sounds like workshop, not main conference). Let me look at CoRL 2024 more carefully:\n- 671 submissions, 39.34% acceptance rate\n\n\n- Scores on a 1-4 scale with an average of 3.01\n\nIt looks like CoRL has been shifting between different scoring scales—1-10 for 2023 and what appears to be 1-4 or 1-5 for 2024. Since I don't have specific guidelines for CoRL 2026, I'll adopt the review dimensions from CoRL 2023 (Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance) and base the scoring on the most recent data available, though I need to determine whether to use the 1-10 or 1-4 scale.\n\nLooking at the actual score ranges, CoRL 2023 shows 4.70-10.00 (clearly 1-10), while CoRL 2024 shows 1.70-4.00 (suggesting 1-5). The review context YAML estimates reviewer averages around 5.5-7.0, which aligns with a 1-10 scale, so I'll stick with that for consistency.\n\nNow I'm setting up the review structure with two reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—followed by a roundtable process that moves through independent scoring, cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis.\n\nI need to establish the CoRL review criteria I'll apply: Originality and Quality, each scored 1-10, covering novelty of the problem and methods alongside technical soundness and experimental rigor.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":812,"cache_read_input_tokens":73907,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens"