{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012DbXjDRv71PdDtyDXTXhbj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tVision-language-action models (VLAs) convert pretrained vision-language backbones into robot controllers through fine-tuning, yet whether the resulting representational changes reflect general training dynamics or architecture-specific artifacts remains unknown.\n2\tWe conduct a systematic cross-architecture analysis of three VLAs built on distinct backbones (Llama-2-7B, Phi-3-mini, Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites, comparing trained and untrained checkpoints with cross-instruction probes, attention profiling, $R^2$ action probes, and counterfactual instruction perturbation.\n3\tWe identify two architecture-independent training effects: $R^2$ exit collapse, where action-relevant information is selectively discarded at exit layers (confirmed in 9 of 10 architecture-task cells), and amplified counterfactual sensitivity, where trained models respond 7--10$\\times$ more strongly to instruction perturbation than untrained counterparts.\n4\tIn contrast, early-layer instruction-dominant attention patterns trace to base-model priors rather than VLA training; one backbone exhibits saturated instruction attention even before fine-tuning.\n5\tThese results compose into a three-tier routing gradient: VLM bases show no instruction routing, general VLA pretraining establishes coarse routing, and task-specific fine-tuning sharpens routing and introduces exit collapse.\n6\tThis gradient suggests that VLA fine-tuning converges on a shared functional organization regardless of backbone architecture.\n7\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"13d9d401-066c-4c16-b394-96fabd1c13b4","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:14:39.961Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VhPuH7yV3SHbjHGtQGvNMA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015XxrNztSvwdcTPbwpUTDcZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/docs/paper/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4649,"cache_read_input_tokens":17481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"5527cd25-4651-4f17-9e4c-bb9dfd9109c5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015XxrNztSvwdcTPbwpUTDcZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tWhat does fine-tuning change inside a Vision-Language-Action model (VLA)?\n5\tVLAs fine-tune large language models on robot demonstrations to produce actions from visual observations and language instructions~\\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla}, but the decoder-only causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions~\\cite{wang2025seeing,pei2025rethinking}.\n6\tInstruction information must reach action-generating positions through learned attention patterns, yet how fine-tuning reshapes these patterns remains uncharacterized.\n7\t\n8\tRecent behavioral studies reveal that VLAs rely disproportionately on visual input~\\cite{grant2026}, with language instructions largely ignored by the action pathway~\\cite{haon2025}.\n9\tThese findings suggest internal specialization but do not explain \\emph{how} instruction information reaches the action-generating readout token, \\emph{what} attention strategy fine-tuning learns compared to language pretraining alone, or \\emph{whether} training creates new representational structure or reconfigures inherited geometry.\n10\tExisting analyses have not combined systematic per-layer probing with factorial trained/pretrained/untrained controls, leaving the representational mechanisms of VLA fine-tuning opaque.\n11\t\n12\tWe address this gap by decomposing OpenVLA-7B~\\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024}, a VLA chaining a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2}, an MLP projector, and Llama-2 7B~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2}.\n13\tWe extract representations from each of 32 LLM layers at both image-token and readout-token positions, and from external baselines (DINOv2~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2}, CLIP~\\cite{radford2021_clip}).\n14\tWe compare three LLM conditions (VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base, and randomly initialized) crossed with instruction presence, and apply four complementary analyses: cross-instruction probes for token-level specialization, L1 attention pattern analysis, RSA~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008} for geometric alignment, and MLP probes~\\cite{alain2017} for functional decodability.\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n17\t\\centering\n18\t\\includegraphics[width=0.88\\linewidth,height=2in,keepaspectratio]{figures/fig_teaser.pdf}\n19\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n20\t\\caption{\\textbf{VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention to entry-exit layers.}\n21\t(a)~Architecture overview.\n22\t(b)~Instruction-dominant heads per layer (L1 attention $>0.5$). Pretrained Llama-2 peaks at mid-depth; VLA-trained OpenVLA concentrates at L1 and L32. Untrained: zero heads at all layers.}\n23\t\\label{fig:teaser}\n24\t\\end{figure}\n25\t\n26\tThree findings emerge.\n27\t\\emph{First}, VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, supported by seven complementary analyses including dose-response and counterfactual ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}).\n28\t\\emph{Second}, a $2{\\times}2$ trained-vs-untrained comparison across two causal-decoder VLAs---OpenVLA (Llama-2) and TraceVLA (Phi-3-V), which start from dramatically different untrained profiles---reveals a consistent reorganization pattern: both reach similar mid-layer action-decodability peaks after training ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.003$), with training effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry (\\S\\ref{sec:nonmonotonic}).\n29\tIf routing were purely architecture-specific, different backbones should not arrive at the same functional profile; the observed consistency across two architectures is compatible with VLA training discovering a common organization constrained by the causal-decoder design.\n30\tArchitecture-expected token specialization (instruction-blind image tokens, perfect readout classification from L1) is verified as a framework sanity check (\\S\\ref{sec:token_specialization}).\n31\t\n32\tOur contributions are: (i)~evidence that VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, consistent across two causal-decoder VLAs, with L1 as the primary hub and redistribution strength modulated by task instruction-discriminability; (ii)~a consistent reorganization pattern across two causal-decoder VLAs with dramatically different starting points, consistent with training discovering a common functional organization, with effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry; and (iii)~a systematic framework for token-level representational analysis of VLAs with factorial controls, including token specialization as a methodology validation.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"89127ef8-7beb-4458-b7b4-cdb04d72dd89","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:14:39.991Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FtgmHhhapaKNxXebFtaLVS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QwqK8g1CfdpQwhUyfi9s6T","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/docs/paper/method.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2525,"cache_read_input_tokens":22130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2525,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"c05f9784-7b85-4637-a417-a363bff62a1d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QwqK8g1CfdpQwhUyfi9s6T","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe investigate how action-relevant structure is distributed across a VLA by combining four complementary analyses: cross-instruction probes, attention pattern analysis, RSA, and functional probes.\n5\t\n6\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n7\t\\subsection{Model and Data}\n8\t\\label{sec:model_data}\n9\t\n10\tWe study OpenVLA-7B~\\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024}, chaining a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2} (DINOv2 ViT-L/14 + SigLIP-SO400M), an MLP projector (2{,}176-D $\\to$ 4{,}096-D), and Llama-2 7B~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} (6.74B parameters) that autoregressively decodes 7-D actions.\n11\tWe use the base checkpoint pretrained on Open X-Embodiment~\\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} (970K episodes spanning multiple robot platforms and task types) and evaluate on LIBERO-Goal~\\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tasks, 50 demonstrations each), which was not part of the pretraining mixture.\n12\tWe compare three LLM conditions: (1)~VLA-trained (released checkpoint), (2)~Llama-2 base (pretrained backbone, no VLA fine-tuning), and (3)~untrained (6.74B parameters reinitialized via \\texttt{from\\_config()}); a fourth condition (task-specific fine-tuning on LIBERO) is compared in \\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}.\n13\tAll three share the same frozen vision encoder and projector.\n14\t\n15\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n16\t\\subsection{Representation Extraction}\n17\t\\label{sec:repr_extraction}\n18\t\n19\tWe extract representations from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline:\n20\t(a)~\\textbf{DinoSigLIP+Projector}, the projector output (4{,}096-D), immediately before LLM processing;\n21\t(b)~\\textbf{LLM image\\_mean}, the mean image-token activation at each of 32 layers;\n22\t(c)~\\textbf{LLM readout}, the hidden state at the final pre-action token position at each layer (located after both image and instruction tokens in the causal sequence, it can attend to all preceding context and is thus the natural site for multimodal integration before action prediction);\n23\t(d)~\\textbf{DINOv2 ViT-L/14}~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\\texttt{cls\\_token}/\\texttt{patch\\_mean}), a self-supervised baseline;\n24\t(e)~\\textbf{CLIP ViT-L/14}~\\cite{radford2021_clip} (\\texttt{patch\\_mean}), a VL-pretrained baseline;\n25\t(f)~\\textbf{Branch-split}, the DINOv2 (1{,}024-D) and SigLIP (1{,}152-D) branches before fusion.\n26\tIn the input sequence, image tokens precede instruction tokens; the causal mask therefore prevents image tokens from attending to instructions by construction.\n27\tThroughout, L0 denotes the embedding layer output, L1--L32 the hidden states after each of the 32 transformer layers, with L32 including the final layer norm.\n28\tWe cross two factors: \\emph{training status} (trained vs.\\ untrained) and \\emph{instruction presence} (with vs.\\ without task description) in a $2\\times 2$ factorial design.\n29\t\n30\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n31\t\\subsection{Cross-Instruction Probes}\n32\t\\label{sec:cross_instruction_probes}\n33\t\n34\tTo test whether different token types encode instruction identity, we construct a cross-instruction dataset: each visual frame is paired with all 10 task instructions, producing 10 representation vectors per frame per layer.\n35\tA linear classifier (10-way) is trained to identify which instruction was present, separately for image\\_mean and readout token positions.\n36\tWe validate with leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) over 10 folds, each holding out one task's instructions, to verify that results reflect generalizable instruction encoding rather than memorization.\n37\t\n38\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t\\subsection{Attention Pattern Analysis}\n40\t\\label{sec:attention_analysis}\n41\t\n42\tWe analyze attention distributions at the readout token position across all 32 layers, categorizing each head as instruction-dominant ($>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens) or image-dominant, with detailed per-head decomposition at L1.\n43\tThis complements the cross-instruction probe by revealing the \\emph{mechanism} through which instruction information reaches the readout token.\n44\t\n45\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n46\t\\subsection{Representational Similarity Analysis}\n47\t\\label{sec:rsa}\n48\t\n49\tWe adopt RSA~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008} to quantify geometric alignment between representations and the action space.\n50\tFor each task, we compute pairwise $N \\times N$ representational dissimilarity matrices (RDMs) using cosine distances for representations and Euclidean distances for 7-D action vectors, using within-task observation pairs from 50 demonstrations.\n51\tAlignment is measured via Spearman $\\rho$ between the upper-triangular entries of the representation and action RDMs; higher $\\rho$ indicates that representationally similar observations require similar actions.\n52\tWe report mean $\\rho$ across 10 tasks with 95\\% confidence intervals from 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples; resampling is performed at the task level ($N=10$), treating each task's pairwise RDM entries as a block to preserve within-task dependency structure~\\cite{nili2014toolbox}.\n53\t\n54\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n55\t\\subsection{Functional Action Probes}\n56\t\\label{sec:probes}\n57\t\n58\tRSA measures geometric alignment but not decodability.\n59\tWe complement it with MLP probes~\\cite{alain2017}: two-layer networks ($d_\\text{in} \\to 256 \\to 7$) trained to predict 7-D action vectors from frozen representations (Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, batch 256, 50 epochs, early stopping patience 5), with demo-level train/test splits (demos 0--39 vs.\\ 40--49).\n60\tWe report per-task macro-averaged $R^2$ across 10 tasks with bootstrap paired comparisons (10{,}000 resamples).\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Why RSA over CKA.}\n63\tRSA captures relative distance structure via scale-invariant Spearman rank correlation, suited to comparing high- and low-dimensional spaces (4{,}096-D vs.\\ 7-D).\n64\tCKA~\\cite{kornblith2019} measures global subspace overlap, which can remain high even when geometric structure differs.\n65\tWe report both in Appendix~\\ref{sec:cka_appendix}; the divergence between metrics (\\S\\ref{sec:nonmonotonic}) indicates neither alone fully characterizes representational structure.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"4368a192-e510-468c-a088-0edc5e5f21ba","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:14:42.880Z"}
