{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015RnYX33YM9v8FWms7h44K9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{array}\n11\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{\\textit{cf.}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{\\textit{et al.}}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization\\\\in Vision-Language-Action Models}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{\n23\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n24\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n25\t}\n26\t\n27\t\\date{}\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{document}\n30\t\n31\t\\maketitle\n32\t\n33\t%% ============================================================\n34\t%%  ABSTRACT\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t\\begin{abstract}\n37\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) fine-tune large language models on robot demonstration data, yet how training reshapes the internal routing of instruction information at the token level---and whether these effects generalize across architectures---remains uncharacterized.\n38\tWe present a systematic token-level analysis framework and apply it to three VLA families built on distinct backbones (Llama-2-7B, Phi-3-mini, Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites (10 architecture$\\times$suite cells).\n39\tWe identify two \\emph{architecture-independent} training signatures: (1) \\textbf{R$^2$ exit collapse}---task-finetuned VLAs show a consistent drop in action-probe R$^2$ from mid-layer peak to exit layer (trained $\\Delta$=0.12--0.69 vs.\\ untrained $\\Delta$=0.01--0.08), confirmed in 9 of 10 cells; and (2) \\textbf{heightened counterfactual sensitivity}---trained models exhibit 7--10$\\times$ greater sensitivity to instruction perturbations than their untrained counterparts.\n40\tA three-tier routing gradient emerges: VLM base models show no action routing, general-purpose VLA pretraining establishes routing without exit collapse, and task-specific fine-tuning adds exit collapse with task-specialized counterfactual patterns.\n41\tWe further distinguish training-induced effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) from base-model-dependent effects (L1 attention head counts), and observe that, under RSA, vision encoder geometry follows an inverted-U profile across LLM layers with functional probes confirming an L8--L13 decodability peak.\n42\tOur framework and findings provide mechanistic grounding for VLA architecture design and training diagnostics.\n43\t\\end{abstract}\n44\t\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n47\t%% ============================================================\n48\t\\section{Introduction}\n49\t\\label{sec:intro}\n50\t\n51\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) repurpose pretrained vision-language models for robot control by fine-tuning on demonstration data~\\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla}.\n52\tWhile behavioral evaluations reveal that VLAs underutilize language instructions~\\cite{grant2026visual, haon2025lost}, the \\emph{internal} mechanisms by which training reshapes instruction routing remain poorly understood.\n53\tPrior work has not decomposed the representational roles of different token positions, explained how fine-tuning reorganizes information flow, or tested whether such effects generalize beyond a single VLA architecture.\n54\t\n55\tWe address these gaps with three contributions:\n56\t\n57\t\\begin{enumerate}\n58\t  \\item \\textbf{A systematic token-level analysis framework} applicable to any decoder-only VLA.\n59\t        The framework combines cross-instruction probing, per-layer attention profiling, representational similarity analysis (RSA), and counterfactual perturbation, validated on 3 architectures $\\times$ 4 task suites (10 cells with full data out of 12 possible).\n60\t\n61\t  \\item \\textbf{Architecture-independent training signatures.}\n62\t        Across OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), and NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B), we find that task-specific fine-tuning consistently produces (a)~R$^2$ exit collapse (mean trained $\\Delta$=0.226, 9/10 cells confirmed) and (b)~heightened counterfactual sensitivity (7--10$\\times$ amplification).\n63\t        We distinguish these \\emph{training-induced} effects from \\emph{base-model-dependent} effects such as L1 attention head counts, which reflect VLM/LLM pretraining rather than VLA fine-tuning.\n64\t\n65\t  \\item \\textbf{A three-tier routing gradient.}\n66\t        Routing \\emph{emerges} at the VLM$\\to$VLA transition (general-purpose pretraining establishes instruction routing without exit collapse) and \\emph{reshapes} at the general$\\to$task-specific transition (task fine-tuning adds exit collapse and task-specialized sensitivity).\n67\t\\end{enumerate}\n68\t\n69\tAs a qualified observation, we report that under RSA, vision encoder geometry follows an inverted-U profile across LLM layers (peak at L12, $\\rho$=0.628), though this finding is metric-dependent: CKA yields a reversed ordering.\n70\tFunctional probes independently confirm a mid-layer decodability peak (L8--L13).\n71\t\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t%%  2. RELATED WORK\n74\t%% ============================================================\n75\t\\section{Related Work}\n76\t\\label{sec:related}\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n79\tVLAs~\\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla} fine-tune vision-language models to predict robot actions conditioned on visual observations and language instructions.\n80\tRecent architectures include OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} (Llama-2-7B backbone), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini backbone), and NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbone with grouped-query attention).\n81\tWhile task performance has improved steadily, the internal mechanisms by which these models integrate multimodal information for action generation are largely unexplored.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Instruction Following in Robotics.}\n84\tGrant \\etal~\\cite{grant2026visual} demonstrated visual dominance in VLA action generation, and Haon \\etal~\\cite{haon2025lost} identified instruction underutilization across multiple VLA families.\n85\tThese behavioral studies establish the phenomenon but do not provide mechanistic explanations at the representational level.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Representational Analysis of Transformers.}\n88\tProbing classifiers~\\cite{belinkov2022probing}, representational similarity analysis~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008representational}, and centered kernel alignment~\\cite{kornblith2019similarity} are standard tools for analyzing transformer internals.\n89\tWe adapt these to the multimodal, autoregressive VLA setting where image, instruction, and action tokens occupy distinct positions and roles within a shared sequence.\n90\t\n91\t%% ============================================================\n92\t%%  3. METHOD\n93\t%% ============================================================\n94\t\\section{Method: Token-Level Analysis Framework}\n95\t\\label{sec:method}\n96\t\n97\tWe propose a framework for systematically analyzing how VLAs process and route instruction information at the token level.\n98\tThe framework operates on decoder-only VLA architectures where the input sequence consists of image tokens, instruction tokens, and action tokens, processed autoregressively.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Factorial Design}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"cb0f87a0-afd2-4cf4-9cd7-0125e22450b0","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:15:03.965Z"}
