{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BV99hpPUZV3diMrCzq9kfS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CpiLFShHySj8ssF9i3tCZ1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T021705/best/paper.tex","offset":100,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4075,"cache_read_input_tokens":68602,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4075,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"a74d25e9-5726-4fc3-8338-4a3bb9d30831"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CpiLFShHySj8ssF9i3tCZ1","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t\\subsection{Factorial Design}\n101\tFor each architecture$\\times$suite cell, we compare three conditions:\n102\t\\begin{itemize}\n103\t  \\item \\textbf{Trained}: task-specific fine-tuned VLA checkpoint.\n104\t  \\item \\textbf{Untrained (OXE-pretrained or VLM base)}: the model before task-specific fine-tuning---either an OXE-pretrained VLA (Tier~2) or a raw VLM (Tier~1).\n105\t  \\item \\textbf{Instruction ablation}: removing or perturbing instruction tokens (empty, wrong task, shuffled, paraphrased).\n106\t\\end{itemize}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Analysis Methods}\n109\t\\label{sec:methods-probes}\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{(1) Cross-Instruction Probing.}\n112\tGiven the same visual frame paired with $K$ distinct instructions, we train a $K$-way classifier on per-layer hidden representations.\n113\tWe extract representations at two token positions: \\texttt{image\\_mean} (mean-pooled image tokens) and \\texttt{last\\_preaction} (last token before action generation).\n114\tLayer-wise accuracy profiles reveal where and how instruction identity becomes decodable.\n115\t\n116\t\\paragraph{(2) Action Probe ($R^2$).}\n117\tA linear probe maps per-layer representations to ground-truth actions.\n118\tPer-layer $R^2$ profiles characterize how action-relevant information distributes across depth, and the difference between peak and exit $R^2$ quantifies \\emph{exit collapse}.\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{(3) Attention Profiling.}\n121\tWe compute per-head attention fractions to instruction vs.\\ image tokens at each layer, classifying heads as instruction-dominant ($>$50\\% attention to instruction tokens) or otherwise.\n122\tThis reveals the layer-wise attention routing pattern.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{(4) Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA).}\n125\tWe compute RSA between VLA layer representations and action-aligned reference representations (from standalone encoders), measuring geometric alignment across depth.\n126\t\n127\t\\paragraph{(5) Counterfactual Perturbation.}\n128\tWe systematically replace instructions (empty, wrong task, shuffled tokens, paraphrased) and measure the resulting shift in hidden representations (Cohen's $d$ for trained models, cosine distance for untrained models that cannot generate meaningful actions).\n129\t\n130\t\\paragraph{(6) CKA Verification.}\n131\tCentered kernel alignment provides a complementary similarity metric to RSA, allowing us to assess metric robustness of geometric findings.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Architectures and Datasets}\n134\tWe evaluate three VLA families spanning different LLM backbones and attention mechanisms:\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{itemize}\n137\t  \\item \\textbf{OpenVLA}: SigLIP+DINOv2 vision encoder, Llama-2-7B backbone (32 layers, multi-head attention).\n138\t  \\item \\textbf{TracVLA}: Phi-3-mini backbone (32 layers, multi-head attention).\n139\t  \\item \\textbf{NORA}: Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbone (36 layers, grouped-query attention, 16 heads / 2 KV heads).\n140\t\\end{itemize}\n141\t\n142\tAll are evaluated on LIBERO benchmark suites: Goal, Spatial, Object, and Long (where checkpoints are available), yielding a 3$\\times$4 matrix with 10 of 12 cells populated (NORA lacks Object and Long fine-tuned checkpoints).\n143\t\n144\t%% ============================================================\n145\t%%  4. EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n146\t%% ============================================================\n147\t\\section{Experiments}\n148\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n149\t\n150\t\\subsection{Token-Level Specialization (Framework Validation)}\n151\t\\label{sec:exp-specialization}\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-invariant.}\n154\tAcross all conditions and all 32 layers, cross-instruction probe accuracy on \\texttt{image\\_mean} tokens remains at chance (10\\% for 10-way classification).\n155\tThis confirms that, as expected from the causal mask, image tokens carry no instruction-specific information---but importantly, this holds even though image and instruction tokens share a residual stream with learned LLM weights.\n156\t\n157\t\\paragraph{Three qualitatively different routing profiles.}\n158\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-instruction} shows the cross-instruction probe accuracy at \\texttt{last\\_preaction} across layers for three conditions on OpenVLA/LIBERO-Goal.\n159\tThe trained model achieves near-perfect accuracy from L1 onward (99.6\\% at L1, 100\\% from L2).\n160\tThe Llama-2 pretrained base reaches 80.2\\% at L1, saturating by L5.\n161\tThe untrained (random init) model starts at 100\\% at L1 but decays to 82\\% by L31---instruction signal degrades through random weights.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\caption{Cross-instruction probe accuracy (\\%) at \\texttt{last\\_preaction} position (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal, 10-way classification). LOOCV: trained 100.00$\\pm$0.00 (L8--L31); untrained decays from 96.64$\\pm$7.07 to 84.76$\\pm$11.59.}\n166\t\\label{tab:cross-instruction}\n167\t\\small\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\tCondition & L0 & L1 & L2 & L5 & L16 & L24 & L31 \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\tTrained          & 10.0 & 99.6 & 100 & 100 & 100  & 100  & 100 \\\\\n173\tLlama-2 base     & 10.0 & 80.2 & 98.7 & 100 & 100  & 100  & 100 \\\\\n174\tUntrained (rand) & 10.0 & 100  & 100 & 99.x & 92.3 & 86.4 & 82.0 \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tTrained \\texttt{img\\_mean} & 10.0 & --- & --- & --- & 10.0 & 10.0 & 9.6 \\\\\n177\t\\bottomrule\n178\t\\end{tabular}\n179\t\\end{table}\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{Six-method systematic validation.}\n182\tTable~\\ref{tab:six-methods} shows that all six probing methods agree on the ordering trained $\\approx$ untrained $\\gg$ DINO across two benchmarks, confirming framework robustness.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\caption{Six probing methods on two LIBERO benchmarks: consistent ordering trained $\\approx$ untrained $\\gg$ DINO. Best per-method scores shown.}\n187\t\\label{tab:six-methods}\n188\t\\small\n189\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n190\t\\toprule\n191\t & Trained Best & Untrained Best & DINO & Ordering \\\\\n192\t\\midrule\n193\tTemporal $\\rho$ & 0.955 & 0.933 & 0.901 & T $\\approx$ U $\\gg$ D \\\\\n194\tAction R$^2$ & 0.683 & 0.617 & 0.687 & T $\\approx$ U $\\gg$ D \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\end{table}\n198\t\n199\t\\subsection{Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization}\n200\t\\label{sec:exp-routing}\n201\t\n202\t\\subsubsection{R$^2$ Exit Collapse}\n203\t\\label{sec:exit-collapse}\n204\t\n205\tTable~\\ref{tab:exit-collapse-matrix} presents the complete 3$\\times$4 R$^2$ exit collapse matrix.\n206\tFor each cell, we report the difference $\\Delta$ between peak and exit R$^2$ at the \\texttt{last\\_preaction} position.\n207\tTask-finetuned models show $\\Delta > 0.08$ in 9 of 10 cells (mean $\\Delta$=0.226, median=0.168).\n208\tUntrained models show negligible collapse (mean $\\Delta$=0.032).\n209\t\n210\t\\begin{table}[t]\n211\t\\centering\n212\t\\caption{R$^2$ exit collapse matrix: trained $\\Delta$ (peak $-$ exit) across 3 architectures $\\times$ 4 LIBERO suites. \\checkmark: $\\Delta > 0.08$; $\\triangle$: OFT outlier. Mean untrained $\\Delta$ = 0.032.}\n213\t\\label{tab:exit-collapse-matrix}\n214\t\\small\n215\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc|c}\n216\t\\toprule\n217\tArchitecture (Backbone) & Goal & Spatial & Object & Long & Mean \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tOpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)   & 0.150 \\checkmark & 0.130 \\checkmark & 0.121 \\checkmark & 0.004 $\\triangle$ & 0.101 \\\\\n220\tTracVLA (Phi-3-mini)    & 0.228 \\checkmark & 0.138 \\checkmark & 0.294 \\checkmark & 0.198 \\checkmark   & 0.215 \\\\\n221\tNORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B)   & 0.691 \\checkmark & 0.302 \\checkmark & ---              & ---                & 0.497 \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tColumn Mean             & 0.356           & 0.190           & 0.208           & 0.101              & \\textbf{0.226} \\\\\n224\t\\bottomrule\n225\t\\end{tabular}\n226\t\\end{table}\n227\t\n228\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA exit collapse detail.}\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla-collapse} shows per-condition R$^2$ profiles for OpenVLA on Goal and Spatial suites.\n230\tTrained models exhibit clear inverted-U profiles: peak at L13--L14, exit R$^2$ dropping to 0.528--0.540.\n231\tOXE-pretrained (untrained) models maintain flat profiles with minimal collapse ($\\Delta$=0.016--0.020), despite higher absolute R$^2$ values (0.825--0.828 at peak), reflecting general-purpose information retention without task-specific exit specialization.\n232\t\n233\t\\begin{table}[t]\n234\t\\centering\n235\t\\caption{OpenVLA R$^2$ exit collapse on Goal and Spatial suites (\\texttt{last\\_preaction}).}\n236\t\\label{tab:openvla-collapse}\n237\t\\small\n238\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n239\t\\toprule\n240\tSuite & Condition & Peak R$^2$ & Peak Layer & Exit R$^2$ (L31) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n241\t\\midrule\n242\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Goal}    & Trained (LIBERO-finetuned) & 0.678 & L13 & 0.528 & 0.150 \\\\\n243\t                          & Untrained (OXE pretrained) & 0.825 & L21 & 0.805 & 0.020 \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Spatial}  & Trained (Spatial-finetuned) & 0.670 & L14 & 0.540 & 0.130 \\\\\n246\t                          & Untrained (OXE pretrained) & 0.828 & L22 & 0.812 & 0.016 \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\t\\paragraph{Full-layer R$^2$ scan.}\n252\tOn LIBERO-Goal alone, we perform a dense 33-layer $\\times$ 2-condition $\\times$ 2-aggregation scan (132 probes; Table~\\ref{tab:r2-full}).\n253\tThe trained \\texttt{image\\_mean} peak occurs at L8 (R$^2$=0.655); trained \\texttt{last\\_preaction} peaks at L13 (R$^2$=0.678).\n254\tUntrained models show peak at L0 for \\texttt{image\\_mean} (0.640) and L32 for \\texttt{last\\_preaction} (0.609), with pathological collapse at L10--L13 (R$^2 \\approx 0$) reflecting random-init LLM's inability to maintain decodable representations at depth.\n255\tBest-vs-best comparison: \\texttt{image\\_mean} $\\Delta$=0.015 ($p$=0.006), \\texttt{last\\_preaction} $\\Delta$=0.069 ($p$<0.0001).\n256\t\n257\t\\begin{table}[t]\n258\t\\centering\n259\t\\caption{Full-layer R$^2$ scan (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal). Peak and exit layer R$^2$ values.}\n260\t\\label{tab:r2-full}\n261\t\\small\n262\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n263\t\\toprule\n264\tCondition & Readout & Peak Layer & Peak R$^2$ & Exit R$^2$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n265\t\\midrule\n266\tTrained   & \\texttt{image\\_mean}       & L8  & 0.655 & $\\sim$0.45 & $\\sim$0.20 \\\\\n267\tTrained   & \\texttt{last\\_preaction}    & L13 & 0.678 & $\\sim$0.55 & $\\sim$0.13 \\\\\n268\tUntrained & \\texttt{image\\_mean}       & L0  & 0.640 & $\\sim$0.35 & $\\sim$0.29 \\\\\n269\tUntrained & \\texttt{last\\_preaction}    & L32 & 0.609 & ---        & baseline \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\end{table}\n273\t\n274\t\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication.}\n275\tOn LIBERO-Spatial (Table~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark}), the trained inverted-U replicates with peaks shifted 2--8 layers deeper (L13 for \\texttt{image\\_mean}, L21 for \\texttt{last\\_preaction}), possibly reflecting deeper processing requirements for spatial reasoning.\n276\tRSA confirms the pattern: trained peak at L14 ($\\rho$=0.691) vs.\\ untrained peak at L0 ($\\rho$=0.652).\n277\t\n278\t\\begin{table}[t]\n279\t\\centering\n280\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark R$^2$ peak comparison (Goal vs.\\ Spatial).}\n281\t\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n282\t\\small\n283\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n284\t\\toprule\n285\tBenchmark & Trained \\texttt{img\\_mean} Peak & Trained \\texttt{lastpre} Peak & Peak Shift \\\\\n286\t\\midrule\n287\tLIBERO-Goal    & L8 (0.655) & L13 (0.678) & --- \\\\\n288\tLIBERO-Spatial & L13 (0.696) & L21 (0.675) & +5 / +8 layers \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\t\\paragraph{OFT outlier.}\n294\tThe sole exception to exit collapse is OpenVLA Long ($\\Delta$=0.004), which uses orthogonal fine-tuning (OFT)---a parameter-efficient method.\n295\tTracVLA Long with full fine-tuning shows $\\Delta$=0.198 on the same suite, ruling out task complexity as the explanation and implicating OFT's restricted parameter update as the cause.\n296\t\n297\t\\subsubsection{Counterfactual Sensitivity}\n298\t\\label{sec:cf}\n299\t\n300\tTable~\\ref{tab:cf-trained} shows counterfactual sensitivity for trained models.\n301\tAcross architectures, the ordering is consistently empty $>$ wrong $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased, with ``empty'' ranking first in 11 of 12 trained settings.\n302\tCohen's $d$ ranges from 0.70 to 1.52, indicating medium-to-large effects.\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[t]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Counterfactual sensitivity: OpenVLA trained vs.\\ untrained (OXE) on Goal and Spatial (Cohen's $d$ for trained, cosine distance for untrained).}\n307\t\\label{tab:cf-trained}\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\tSuite & Condition & empty & wrong & shuffled & para \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Goal}    & Trained   & $d$=0.96 & $d$=0.91 & $d$=0.77 & $d$=0.70 \\\\\n314\t                          & OXE untr. & $d$=1.05 & $d$=0.84 & $d$=0.63 & $d$=0.61 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Spatial}  & Trained   & $d$=0.94 & $d$=0.85 & $d$=0.83 & $d$=0.81 \\\\\n317\t                          & OXE untr. & $d$=1.02 & $d$=0.82 & $d$=0.61 & $d$=0.59 \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\end{table}\n321\t\n322\t\\paragraph{Untrained counterfactual comparison.}\n323\tTable~\\ref{tab:cf-untrained} compares untrained counterfactual responses across architectures.\n324\tVLM bases (Tier~1: Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) show weak sensitivity (cosine distance $<$ 0.2), while OXE-pretrained models (Tier~2) show moderate sensitivity.\n325\tThe ordering reversal between trained (wrong $>$ shuffled) and untrained VLM (shuffled $>$ wrong) suggests training induces semantic rather than positional sensitivity.\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{table}[t]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\caption{Untrained counterfactual responses (cosine distance). Tier~1 = VLM base; Tier~2 = OXE pretrained. *~denotes reference values from other cells.}\n330\t\\label{tab:cf-untrained}\n331\t\\small\n332\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n333\t\\toprule\n334\tModel & Suite & empty & shuffled & wrong & para & Tier \\\\\n335\t\\midrule\n336\tOXE (Llama-2)   & Object  & 0.085 & 0.045 & 0.052 & 0.025 & 2 \\\\\n337\tPhi-3-Vision     & Spatial & 0.148 & 0.078 & 0.018 & 0.005 & 1 \\\\\n338\tPhi-3-Vision     & Object  & 0.142 & 0.072 & 0.015 & 0.004 & 1 \\\\\n339\tQwen2.5-VL       & Goal*   & 0.081 & 0.033 & 0.014 & 0.004 & 1 \\\\\n340\t\\bottomrule\n341\t\\end{tabular}\n342\t\\end{table}\n343\t\n344\t\\subsubsection{L1 Attention Routing: Base-Model-Dependent}\n345\t\\label{sec:l1-heads}\n346\t\n347\tTable~\\ref{tab:l1-heads} presents L1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures.\n348\tThe pattern is clear: L1 routing reflects the base model's pretraining, \\emph{not} VLA fine-tuning.\n349\tTracVLA's untrained model (Phi-3-Vision) already has 32/32 instruction-dominant heads; training \\emph{reduces} this to 27--30/32 as some heads are repurposed.\n350\tNORA shows stability (13--14/16 untrained vs.\\ 12--13/16 trained).\n351\tOnly OpenVLA with random initialization (0/32) shows apparent training-induced L1 routing, but OXE-pretrained OpenVLA already has 25/32, confirming the effect originates from OXE pretraining, not task fine-tuning.\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[t]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{L1 instruction-dominant heads: trained vs.\\ untrained across architectures. L1 routing is base-model-dependent, not training-induced.}\n356\t\\label{tab:l1-heads}\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\tArchitecture & Trained & Untrained & Training-Induced? & Source \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tOpenVLA (random init) & 24/32 & 0/32  & Appears so & No LLM pretraining \\\\\n363\tOpenVLA (OXE pretr.)  & 24/32 & 25/32 & No         & OXE pretraining \\\\\n364\tTracVLA               & 27--30/32 & 32/32 & No (decreased) & Phi-3 pretraining \\\\\n365\tNORA                  & 12--13/16 & 13--14/16 & No (stable) & Qwen2.5-VL pretraining \\\\\n366\t\\bottomrule\n367\t\\end{tabular}\n368\t\\end{table}\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA L1 attention detail.}\n371\tFor the OpenVLA trained model, 24/32 heads are instruction-dominant with a mean text attention fraction of 57.7\\%, compared to 12\\% for untrained (random init) and 36.5\\% for Llama-2 base.\n372\tThe attention gradient---0/32 $\\to$ 9/32 (Llama-2 base) $\\to$ 24/32 (VLA trained)---tracks the progressive accumulation of language processing capability through pretraining stages, but the OXE comparison (25/32 untrained OXE $\\to$ 24/32 trained) shows this is not a fine-tuning effect.\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Three-Tier Routing Gradient}\n375\t\\label{sec:three-tier}\n376\t\n377\tTable~\\ref{tab:three-tier} synthesizes the three-tier gradient that emerges from comparing VLM bases (Tier~1), OXE-pretrained VLAs (Tier~2), and task-finetuned VLAs (Tier~3).\n378\t\n379\t\\begin{table}[t]\n380\t\\centering\n381\t\\caption{Three-tier routing gradient. Routing emerges at the VLM$\\to$VLA transition (Tier~1$\\to$2) and reshapes with task specialization (Tier~2$\\to$3).}\n382\t\\label{tab:three-tier}\n383\t\\small\n384\t\\begin{tabular}{lp{2.4cm}cccc}\n385\t\\toprule\n386\tTier & Representative & Exit Collapse & CF (empty) & L1 Routing & Interp. \\\\\n387\t\\midrule\n388\t1: VLM base      & Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL & None ($\\Delta < 0.05$)       & cos $<$ 0.2 & From LLM pretr. & No action routing \\\\\n389\t2: OXE VLA       & openvla-7b (970K ep.)     & None ($\\Delta$ = 0.02)       & $d \\approx 1.05$ & 25/32 from OXE  & General routing \\\\\n390\t3: Task VLA      & All 3 finetuned           & Yes ($\\Delta$ = 0.12--0.69)  & $d$ = 0.7--1.5   & $\\approx$ Tier~2 & Task-specific \\\\\n391\t\\bottomrule\n392\t\\end{tabular}\n393\t\\end{table}\n394\t\n395\tThe gradient reveals that routing is not created wholesale by task fine-tuning.\n396\tOXE pretraining on 970K diverse episodes already establishes strong L1 routing (25/32 heads) and substantial counterfactual sensitivity ($d$=1.05) without exit collapse ($\\Delta$=0.020).\n397\tTask-specific fine-tuning then \\emph{reshapes} this existing routing: it induces exit collapse (action-relevant information selectively dropped at exit layers) and specializes counterfactual patterns to the target task, while L1 head counts remain approximately constant.\n398\t\n399\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA Goal three-tier detail.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"a6b07444-bbf7-4b29-b093-477d9f0bd495","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:15:08.706Z"}
