{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ta7NbYzs7wcYjJLUrpDMGR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JVkK1SZSHiATv8przunRg2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/docs/paper/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5370,"cache_read_input_tokens":39075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5370,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015aqnmHzHu27rYALNnjZ4kn","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"74f27053-8ab3-4f5d-8f67-89fc9514261c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JVkK1SZSHiATv8przunRg2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related_work}\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Vision-language-action models.}\n4\tVLAs fine-tune large language models on robot demonstrations to produce action tokens conditioned on visual observations and language instructions~\\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024,black2024_pi0}; recent work conditions action generation on dynamic visual reweighting~\\cite{xiao2025ava}.\n5\tA central assumption is that fine-tuning injects action-relevant knowledge into pretrained representations; unlike NLP instruction tuning, which targets behavioral compliance~\\cite{wei2022finetuned,ouyang2022training}, we test this at the representational level through per-layer, per-token-type decomposition.\n6\tFine-tuning operates in a low intrinsic dimension~\\cite{aghajanyan2021intrinsic} and can distort pretrained features~\\cite{kumar2022finetuning}; our per-layer analysis reveals where restructuring concentrates and finds VLA training primarily redistributes rather than distorts, with image geometry largely preserved ($\\Delta \\leq 1.7\\%$).\n7\t\n8\t\\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretability in transformers.}\n9\tMechanistic analyses have identified residual stream communication~\\cite{elhage2021}, induction heads for in-context learning~\\cite{olsson2022}, complete circuits for specific tasks~\\cite{wang2023_ioi,conmy2023}, causal tracing for factual recall~\\cite{meng2022_rome}, and the tuned lens for per-layer prediction~\\cite{belrose2023_tunedlens}, while feature-level methods have scaled to dictionary learning over millions of features~\\cite{bills2023language,templeton2024scaling}.\n10\tAttention probing reveals that individual heads encode syntactic relations~\\cite{clark2019}, capture depth-dependent patterns~\\cite{vig2019}, and that a small subset accounts for most performance~\\cite{voita2019}; Michel et al.~\\cite{michel2019sixteen} showed most heads can be pruned without loss.\n11\tOur L1 analysis extends this to VLAs: we use functional ablation to study attention redistribution and find that 24 of 32 heads become instruction-dominant after fine-tuning, with progressive ablation yielding monotonic rather than threshold-like effects.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Behavioral and mechanistic analysis of VLAs.}\n14\tBehavioral evaluations reveal that current VLAs rely disproportionately on visual input~\\cite{grant2026,hou2026,fei2025}.\n15\tMechanistic studies have identified sparse semantic directions linked to action selection~\\cite{haon2025}, interpretable features and memorized sequences~\\cite{swann2026,buurmeijer2026}, symbolic state representations~\\cite{lu2025,molinari2025}, and fine-tuning erosion of VLM reasoning~\\cite{fang2025}.\n16\tThe closest works---Grant et al.~\\cite{grant2026} (visual dominance) and H\\\"aon et al.~\\cite{haon2025} (sparse directions)---address \\emph{what} information VLAs encode; our analysis is complementary, addressing \\emph{where} instruction information is redistributed across layers and token positions, with trained-vs-untrained controls that isolate fine-tuning effects.\n17\t\n18\t\\paragraph{Token-level functional specialization in transformers.}\n19\tIn Vision Transformers, the CLS token aggregates global information~\\cite{dosovitskiy2021} and certain patch positions serve as registers~\\cite{darcet2024}; in language models, feed-forward layers store factual associations in position-specific patterns~\\cite{geva2021}, probing reveals increasingly abstract features across depth~\\cite{tenney2019}, the logit lens~\\cite{nostalgebraist2020} tracks prediction evolution, and Neo et al.~\\cite{neo2025} identified task-specific neurons in multi-task LLMs.\n20\tIn VLMs, recent work has characterized visual token sparsity and redundancy~\\cite{fan2026visual}, progressive visual-textual alignment across layers~\\cite{neo2025interpreting,long2026understanding}, and phase-structured feed-forward dynamics~\\cite{wei2024phase}.\n21\tWe extend this to VLAs: image tokens remain instruction-blind while the readout token serves as the primary multimodal hub.\n22\t\n23\t\\paragraph{Representation similarity and probing methodology.}\n24\tRSA~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008}, SVCCA~\\cite{raghu2017}, CCA~\\cite{morcos2018}, and Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)~\\cite{kornblith2019} compare geometric structure between representation spaces; Raghu et al.~\\cite{raghu2021} applied these tools to compare ViT and CNN representations.\n25\tLinear probes~\\cite{alain2017,hewitt2019,hewitt2019control,belinkov2022} complement geometric methods with functional evaluation.\n26\tWe adopt RSA and MLP probes, with CKA in the appendix.\n27\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015aqnmHzHu27rYALNnjZ4kn","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"4c3a1ba7-0949-47c4-8b97-f78813523c93","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:15:12.010Z"}
