{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019pdXshEWh8xKs9YvJs4rcp","type":"tool_result","content":"400\tOn LIBERO-Goal, the gradient is particularly clear (Table~\\ref{tab:three-tier-goal}): Tier~1 (random init) has no routing capability (0/32 L1 heads, pathological R$^2$ collapse at L10--L13), Tier~2 (OXE) has established routing (25/32 heads, $d$=1.05, no exit collapse), and Tier~3 (task-finetuned) has specialized routing (24/32 heads, $d$=0.96, exit collapse $\\Delta$=0.150).\n401\t\n402\t\\begin{table}[t]\n403\t\\centering\n404\t\\caption{Three-tier routing gradient on LIBERO-Goal (OpenVLA).}\n405\t\\label{tab:three-tier-goal}\n406\t\\small\n407\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n408\t\\toprule\n409\tTier & Model & L1 Heads & CF $d$ (empty) & R$^2$ Exit $\\Delta$ & Collapse? \\\\\n410\t\\midrule\n411\t1 (VLM base) & Llama-2-7B random init & 0/32   & $\\sim$0  & N/A (pathological) & N/A \\\\\n412\t2 (OXE VLA)  & openvla-7b             & 25/32  & 1.05     & 0.020             & No \\\\\n413\t3 (Task VLA) & openvla-goal-finetuned & 24/32  & 0.96     & 0.150             & Yes \\\\\n414\t\\bottomrule\n415\t\\end{tabular}\n416\t\\end{table}\n417\t\n418\t\\subsection{Vision Encoder Geometry}\n419\t\\label{sec:encoder-geometry}\n420\t\n421\t\\subsubsection{Encoder-to-VLA RSA Comparison}\n422\t\\label{sec:rsa-encoder}\n423\t\n424\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-encoder} shows RSA between standalone encoders and action-aligned reference representations.\n425\tUnder RSA, the frozen SigLIP+projector retains the strongest geometric alignment ($\\rho$=0.615, 95\\% CI [0.526, 0.695]).\n426\tLLM processing degrades this alignment: the VLA trained peak ($\\rho$=0.488) and untrained peak ($\\rho$=0.485) are nearly identical, with a degradation magnitude of 0.127.\n427\tDINOv2 cls\\_token ($\\rho$=0.388) serves as a vision-only baseline.\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[t]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{Encoder-to-VLA RSA comparison. LLM processing degrades geometric alignment from the frozen encoder; training does not recover it (trained $\\approx$ untrained). DINOv2 establishes a vision-only floor.}\n432\t\\label{tab:rsa-encoder}\n433\t\\small\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\tSource & RSA $\\rho$ & 95\\% CI & vs.\\ DINO $\\Delta$ \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\tSigLIP+Proj       & 0.615 & [0.526, 0.695] & +0.227 \\\\\n439\tVLA Trained Peak   & 0.488 & ---            & +0.100 \\\\\n440\tVLA Untrained Peak & 0.485 & ---            & +0.097 \\\\\n441\tDINO cls\\_token    & 0.388 & ---            & baseline \\\\\n442\t\\bottomrule\n443\t\\end{tabular}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t\n446\t\\subsubsection{Per-Layer RSA Profile}\n447\t\\label{sec:rsa-profile}\n448\t\n449\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-profile} shows the per-layer RSA profile.\n450\tTrained models exhibit an inverted-U: RSA rises from L0 (0.612) to a peak at L12 (0.628, $\\Delta$=+0.016) before declining to L32 (0.422, $\\Delta$=$-$0.206).\n451\tUntrained models show monotonic decay from L0 (0.612) to L32 (0.545).\n452\tBoth conditions share the same L0 starting point because they share a frozen projector.\n453\tThe projector-to-L1 transition produces the largest single drop (0.13--0.25), corresponding to the embedding dimension transformation.\n454\t\n455\t\\begin{table}[t]\n456\t\\centering\n457\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA profile summary (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal, \\texttt{image\\_mean}).}\n458\t\\label{tab:rsa-profile}\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n461\t\\toprule\n462\tCondition & L0 & Peak Layer & Peak RSA & L32 & $\\Delta$(peak--exit) \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\tTrained \\texttt{img\\_mean}   & 0.612 & L12 & 0.628 & 0.422 & 0.206 \\\\\n465\tUntrained \\texttt{img\\_mean} & 0.612 & L0  & 0.612 & 0.545 & 0.067 \\\\\n466\t\\bottomrule\n467\t\\end{tabular}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\t\\subsubsection{Instruction Invariance of RSA}\n471\t\n472\tThe 2$\\times$2 training $\\times$ instruction matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:2x2-rsa}) confirms that RSA is invariant to both training and instruction conditions, converging within a 0.48--0.49 band (bootstrap $\\Delta$ CI includes 0).\n473\tExtended to four instruction conditions (normal, random words, shuffled, empty), the maximum absolute deviation is 0.017, establishing triple invariance: RSA measures purely visual structure.\n474\t\n475\t\\begin{table}[t]\n476\t\\centering\n477\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 RSA convergence matrix. All four conditions converge within [0.48, 0.49]; neither training nor instruction presence has a significant effect (bootstrap CI includes 0).}\n478\t\\label{tab:2x2-rsa}\n479\t\\small\n480\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n481\t\\toprule\n482\t & With Instruction & No Instruction & Instruction Effect \\\\\n483\t\\midrule\n484\tTrained   & 0.488 & 0.489 & $-$0.001 \\\\\n485\tUntrained & 0.485 & 0.480 & +0.004 \\\\\n486\t\\midrule\n487\tTraining Effect & +0.003 & +0.009 & \\\\\n488\t\\bottomrule\n489\t\\end{tabular}\n490\t\\end{table}\n491\t\n492\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Encoder Comparison}\n493\t\\label{sec:cross-encoder}\n494\t\n495\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-encoder} compares RSA and R$^2$ across encoder types.\n496\tUnder RSA, DINOv2 cls ($\\rho$=0.692) outranks SigLIP+Proj ($\\rho$=0.615) and CLIP ViT-L ($\\rho$=0.557), suggesting that self-supervised spatial features provide stronger action-aligned geometry than vision-language contrastive features.\n497\t\n498\t\\begin{table}[t]\n499\t\\centering\n500\t\\caption{Cross-encoder comparison (RSA and action-probe R$^2$).}\n501\t\\label{tab:cross-encoder}\n502\t\\small\n503\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n504\t\\toprule\n505\tEncoder & RSA & R$^2$ (action) & Ranking \\\\\n506\t\\midrule\n507\tDINOv2 cls       & 0.692 / 0.631 & 0.634 & 1st / 2nd \\\\\n508\tSigLIP+Proj      & 0.615         & 0.630 & 2nd / 3rd \\\\\n509\tCLIP ViT-L       & 0.557         & 0.587 & 4th / 5th \\\\\n510\t\\bottomrule\n511\t\\end{tabular}\n512\t\\end{table}\n513\t\n514\t\\paragraph{Branch-split decomposition.}\n515\tWithin the fused backbone (DINOv2 + SigLIP), branch-split RSA (Table~\\ref{tab:branch-split}) shows equal contribution from both branches (0.619 vs.\\ 0.621), with fusion providing a complementary gain (+0.015) and the projector introducing a small compression loss ($-$0.019).\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[t]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\caption{Branch-split RSA decomposition of the fused backbone.}\n520\t\\label{tab:branch-split}\n521\t\\small\n522\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n523\t\\toprule\n524\tComponent & RSA & Note \\\\\n525\t\\midrule\n526\tDINOv2 branch (1088d)  & 0.619 & in-backbone \\\\\n527\tSigLIP branch (1088d)  & 0.621 & in-backbone \\\\\n528\tFused (2176d)          & 0.634 & complementary gain \\\\\n529\tPost-projector (4096d) & 0.615 & compression loss $-$0.019 \\\\\n530\t\\bottomrule\n531\t\\end{tabular}\n532\t\\end{table}\n533\t\n534\t\\subsubsection{Metric Robustness: RSA vs.\\ CKA}\n535\t\\label{sec:metric-robustness}\n536\t\n537\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-cka} reveals an important metric dissociation.\n538\tCKA yields an ordering reversal: untrained $>$ trained $>$ projector $>$ DINO, while RSA gives projector $>$ trained $\\approx$ untrained $>$ DINO.\n539\tDINO ranks lowest under both metrics---a robust finding.\n540\tThe dissociation implies that LLM training degrades local geometric alignment (RSA) while preserving or enhancing global structure (CKA).\n541\tThis ``geometric degradation + structural preservation'' is richer than either metric alone would suggest, and all geometric claims in this paper are explicitly qualified by metric.\n542\t\n543\t\\begin{table}[t]\n544\t\\centering\n545\t\\caption{RSA vs.\\ CKA ordering comparison. Ordering reversal between metrics reflects different aspects of representational structure.}\n546\t\\label{tab:rsa-cka}\n547\t\\small\n548\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n549\t\\toprule\n550\tSource & RSA Rank & Linear CKA Rank & RDM-CKA Rank \\\\\n551\t\\midrule\n552\tSigLIP+Proj  & 1st (0.615) & 3rd (0.081) & 2nd (0.257) \\\\\n553\tVLA Trained   & 2nd (0.488) & 4th (0.083) & 1st (0.260) \\\\\n554\tVLA Untrained & 3rd (0.485) & 2nd (0.089) & 3rd (0.246) \\\\\n555\tDINO          & 4th (0.388) & 5th (0.066) & 4th (0.241) \\\\\n556\t\\bottomrule\n557\t\\end{tabular}\n558\t\\end{table}\n559\t\n560\t\\subsubsection{Weight Analysis and Frozen Encoder Verification}\n561\t\\label{sec:weight-analysis}\n562\t\n563\tThe vision encoder and projector remain perfectly frozen during training (cosine similarity = 1.0 and 1.001, respectively).\n564\tLLM layers show uniform relative weight change (1.22--1.37$\\times$) across all 32 layers, with the output head at only 1.12$\\times$---ruling out shallow adaptation (threshold: 3$\\times$).\n565\tThis confirms that training-induced effects emerge from distributed LLM-wide reorganization, not localized head or layer modifications.\n566\t\n567\t%% ============================================================\n568\t%%  5. DISCUSSION\n569\t%% ============================================================\n570\t\\section{Discussion}\n571\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n572\t\n573\t\\paragraph{What VLA training does and does not do.}\n574\tOur cross-architecture analysis reveals a clean separation between training-induced effects and inherited properties.\n575\tVLA fine-tuning \\emph{does} create R$^2$ exit collapse and enhance counterfactual sensitivity---these are architecture-independent signatures observable across Llama-2-7B, Phi-3-mini, and Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbones.\n576\tVLA fine-tuning does \\emph{not} create L1 attention routing: this is inherited from the base model's pretraining (language modeling, vision-language alignment, or OXE pretraining), and task fine-tuning may even reduce the number of instruction-dominant heads.\n577\t\n578\t\\paragraph{The routing gradient.}\n579\tThe three-tier gradient---VLM base $\\to$ OXE VLA $\\to$ task VLA---suggests that instruction routing is progressively refined rather than created wholesale.\n580\tOXE pretraining establishes general-purpose routing (L1 heads, counterfactual sensitivity) without task specialization (no exit collapse).\n581\tTask fine-tuning then reshapes this routing, concentrating action-relevant information in mid-layers while allowing exit layers to specialize for action output.\n582\tThis distinction has practical implications: diagnostic tools can use exit collapse as a signature that task-specific routing has been learned.\n583\t\n584\t\\paragraph{Metric dependence of geometric findings.}\n585\tOur RSA and CKA results diverge on encoder rankings and LLM-layer trajectories.\n586\tRSA captures local geometric alignment (which LLM processing degrades), while CKA captures global structural preservation (which LLM processing maintains).\n587\tWe present this not as a limitation but as a richer characterization: VLA training simultaneously degrades fine-grained geometric alignment and preserves coarse structural organization.\n588\tAll geometric claims in this paper are explicitly qualified by the metric used.\n589\t\n590\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n591\tOur analysis is conducted in an open-loop setting without closed-loop robot evaluation, limiting claims about behavioral consequences.\n592\tThe LIBERO benchmark, while diverse, uses a fixed embodiment; generalization to other platforms remains untested.\n593\tNORA's 2-of-4 suite coverage (no Object/Long checkpoints) leaves gaps in the matrix.\n594\tThe OFT outlier (OpenVLA Long, $\\Delta$=0.004) suggests that parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods may circumvent the exit collapse signature, warranting further investigation.\n595\t\n596\t%% ============================================================\n597\t%%  6. CONCLUSION\n598\t%% ============================================================\n599\t\\section{Conclusion}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"3b0c68ef-8f83-487d-b746-bdfc3ee8cf04","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:15:15.096Z"}
