{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EVjzVDHQygz8egi8Fb3hZK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qj8H4FFEzPATi1KDekF5DH","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Focus\n\n## Paper: \"Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a token-level analysis framework for understanding how VLA fine-tuning reshapes internal instruction routing, applied to three VLA families (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites (10 of 12 cells populated). The authors identify two architecture-independent training signatures—R² exit collapse (9/10 cells) and heightened counterfactual sensitivity (7–10× amplification)—and distinguish these from base-model-dependent effects (L1 attention head counts). A three-tier routing gradient (VLM base → OXE-pretrained → task-finetuned) is proposed as an organizing principle.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Rigorous factorial experimental design.** The 3-architecture × 4-suite matrix with trained/untrained conditions is well-structured. The paper systematically distinguishes training-induced effects from inherited base-model properties—a methodological contribution that preempts the obvious confound (\"are you just measuring pretraining?\"). The OXE-pretrained vs. task-finetuned vs. random-init three-way comparison for OpenVLA is particularly informative.\n\n**S2. Multiple converging analysis methods with appropriate controls.** The combination of cross-instruction probing, attention profiling, R² action probes, counterfactual perturbation, RSA, and CKA provides triangulation. The paper does not rely on a single metric. The dose-response ablation (4→32 heads, monotonic d=0.48→0.64), random-subset specificity control (z=−3.11), threshold sensitivity analysis (30%–70%), and TOST equivalence testing all demonstrate methodological care beyond what is typical for analysis papers in this area.\n\n**S3. Honest treatment of metric dependence.** The RSA vs. CKA ordering reversal is reported transparently rather than buried. The paper explicitly qualifies geometric claims by metric and acknowledges the inverted-U profile as metric-dependent. This is a model of how to handle conflicting metrics in representational analysis.\n\n**S4. Statistical rigor.** Bootstrap CIs with task-level blocking, Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction surviving at 29-30/33 layers, global Bonferroni correction preserving 40/45 significant tests (88.9%), power analysis acknowledging underpowered RSA comparisons, TOST equivalence testing for cross-architecture convergence—these collectively exceed the statistical standard in the robot learning literature.\n\n**S5. Cross-architecture consistency as a genuine finding.** R² exit collapse confirmed in 9/10 cells across three architecturally distinct backbones (MHA vs. GQA, 3B–7B parameters) is a strong empirical result. The OFT outlier is explained mechanistically (parameter-efficient fine-tuning), not dismissed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. All analyses are open-loop; no closed-loop behavioral validation.** This is the most significant limitation. The paper demonstrates that instruction-dominant heads influence action *representations* (trajectory divergence d=0.65) but shows that ablating these heads does not increase ground-truth MSE (Δ≈0). This creates an interpretive puzzle: if ablating 24/32 instruction-dominant heads at L1 does not degrade action quality, what is the functional role of the identified routing? The planned behavioral_pilot experiments (L1 head ablation → success rate) are listed as \"pending\" in the project. Without closed-loop evidence, the paper's claims about \"instruction routing\" being functionally important remain indirect. The authors acknowledge this limitation but it substantially weakens the practical significance for a robotics venue.\n\n**W2. Incomplete matrix coverage.** 10/12 cells is good but the gaps matter: NORA lacks Object and Long, meaning the smallest/most architecturally distinct model (GQA, 36 layers, 16 heads) has only 2 cells. The asymmetry in untrained conditions (OpenVLA uses random init *or* OXE-pretrained; TracVLA uses Phi-3-Vision; NORA uses Qwen2.5-VL) makes direct cross-architecture comparison non-trivial. Different \"untrained\" baselines measure different things—random init has no language capability, OXE-pretrained has seen 970K robot episodes, and VLM bases have vision-language but no robot data. The three-tier gradient is primarily demonstrated on OpenVLA alone; the other architectures lack the Tier-2 (OXE) condition entirely.\n\n**W3. The \"architecture-independent\" claim is tested on architecturally similar models.** All three are decoder-only causal transformers. The paper's own Discussion notes that cross-attention VLAs (π₀, Octo) should lack L1 instruction-dominant heads—but these architectures are not tested. The claim of architecture independence is more accurately \"decoder-only architecture independence.\" Given that the field is moving toward diffusion-based and cross-attention VLAs, the generalizability of findings to the architectures that matter most going forward is uncertain.\n\n**W4. Counterfactual sensitivity metric inconsistency between trained and untrained.** Trained models use Cohen's d on action outputs; untrained VLM bases use cosine distance on hidden representations (because untrained VLMs cannot generate meaningful actions). This makes the \"7–10× amplification\" claim hard to interpret quantitatively—the metrics are not on the same scale. The paper acknowledges this methodological difference but still headlines the \"7–10×\" number prominently. A fairer comparison would restrict to the OXE-pretrained condition where both action-output d values are available (Table 7 shows OXE Goal: d=1.05 vs. trained Goal: d=0.96—actually *lower* for trained), which tells a different story than \"7–10× amplification.\"\n\n**W5. R² exit collapse interpretation is underspecified.** The paper identifies the phenomenon (peak-to-exit R² drop) but does not explain *why* it occurs or what function it serves. Is it beneficial (exit layers specializing for action tokenization)? Detrimental (information loss)? Neutral (orthogonal rotation)? The OFT outlier (Δ=0.004) achieves functional VLA performance without exit collapse, suggesting it may not be necessary. Without connecting exit collapse to downstream task performance, it remains a descriptive observation rather than a mechanistic insight.\n\n**W6. Probe methodology concerns.** The MLP probe (input→256→7) is acknowledged as overparameterized (N/params ≈ 0.06). While the linear probe replication is appreciated, the primary results all use the MLP probe. The train/test split (demos 0–39 vs. 40–49) is fixed rather than cross-validated, and demo-level splitting may not fully prevent information leakage if consecutive demos share similar states. The 50-epoch training with early stopping on a fixed split could overfit to the test set boundary.\n\n**W7. Table 2 (six-method validation) shows only 2 of 6 methods.** The paper claims \"six probing methods agree\" but only shows temporal ρ and action R². The other four methods (presumably including cross-instruction classification, RSA, CKA, and something else) are relegated to an unspecified appendix location. For a claim about methodological triangulation, showing all six is important.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The ablation finding is surprising: removing 24/32 instruction-dominant heads *increases* R² by +0.030 and does not increase MSE. Does this mean the identified \"instruction routing\" is not functionally necessary for action prediction? How do you reconcile this with the claim that these heads \"route fine-grained spatial information\"?\n\n**Q2.** For the three-tier gradient, you have the full Tier 1→2→3 comparison only for OpenVLA (random init → OXE → task-finetuned). TracVLA and NORA only have Tier 1→3 (VLM base → task-finetuned). Can you comment on whether the Tier-2 intermediate (general VLA pretraining without task specialization) is truly architecture-independent, or is this an OpenVLA-specific observation?\n\n**Q3.** The OFT outlier (Δ=0.004) is a single data point but potentially very informative. If OFT avoids exit collapse while maintaining task performance, does this suggest exit collapse is a *side effect* of full fine-tuning rather than a *functional signature* of task routing? Have you checked OpenVLA-Long task success rates to confirm comparable performance?\n\n**Q4.** You compare trained vs. untrained counterfactual sensitivity using different metrics (Cohen's d vs. cosine distance). Could you provide Cohen's d for the OXE-pretrained condition (which *can* generate actions) to enable a same-metric comparison? Table 7 seems to show this for Goal (OXE d=1.05 for empty), which is actually *higher* than trained (d=0.96)—does this contradict the \"heightened sensitivity\" claim?\n\n**Q5.** The cross-architecture convergence result (OpenVLA L9 R²=0.637 vs. TracVLA L11 R²=0.634, Δ=0.003) uses a TOST equivalence test with ε=0.05. How sensitive is this result to the choice of equivalence bound? At ε=0.02 or ε=0.01, does equivalence still hold?\n\n**Q6.** All experiments use LIBERO with a single robot embodiment. LIBERO tasks are relatively short-horizon and well-structured. Do you expect the identified routing patterns to hold for longer-horizon tasks or different embodiments?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Detailed Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Quality** | 6 | Strong statistical methodology and multi-method triangulation. However, the open-loop limitation is significant: key claims about \"routing\" lack behavioral grounding. The metric inconsistency in CF comparisons (W4) and the surprising ablation result (Q1) create interpretive tensions that are not fully resolved. |\n| **Clarity** | 7 | Well-organized with clear factorial design. Tables and figures are informative. The two-version issue (docs/paper vs. dream paper) suggests the paper is in transition between framings, but the content I reviewed is clearly written. Appendix is thorough. The paper would benefit from a clearer \"what this means for practitioners\" takeaway. |\n| **Significance** | 5 | The cross-architecture analysis is novel and the framework is reusable. However, as a purely analytical paper with no new method and no behavioral validation, the practical impact for the robotics community is limited. The main actionable insight—exit collapse as a training diagnostic—is interesting but narrow. The finding that L1 heads are inherited rather than trained is important but somewhat deflationary (it means a previously claimed \"training effect\" is actually a pretraining artifact). |\n| **Relevance** | 6 | Directly about VLA models for robot learning, which is core CoRL territory. The analysis framework targets a real gap—understanding what fine-tuning does inside VLAs. However, the lack of closed-loop validation and the restriction to decoder-only architectures (while the field moves toward diffusion/flow-matching) somewhat limit the relevance to current practice. |\n| **Overall** | 6 | Weak accept. This is a thorough, carefully conducted analysis paper with genuine methodological contributions (factorial design, multi-metric triangulation, training-vs-inherited decomposition). The cross-architecture R² exit collapse finding is convincing. However, the absence of behavioral validation, the metric inconsistency in the headline CF claim, and the limited architectural scope (decoder-only only) prevent a stronger recommendation. The paper would benefit from (1) at least one closed-loop experiment showing exit collapse correlates with task performance, (2) a same-metric CF comparison, and (3) more explicit discussion of what practitioners should *do* with these findings. |\n\n## 6. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** (Confident but not certain). I have expertise in transformer interpretability and representational analysis. I have carefully read the main text and full appendix. My uncertainty is primarily about (a) whether the CoRL community values pure analysis papers at this depth, and (b) whether the behavioral experiments in preparation would substantially change the paper's impact if included.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- Table 2 caption says \"six probing methods\" but only two rows are shown. Either show all six or change the caption.\n- The \"untrained\" condition means different things across architectures (random init, OXE-pretrained, VLM base). A clearer taxonomy in the Method section would help.\n- The paper title in docs/paper/main.tex (\"Three Architectures, One Routing Pattern\") differs from the dream version (\"Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization\"). The latter is more precise.\n- The NORA architecture uses grouped-query attention with only 2 KV heads per group. The attention head analysis (instruction-dominant classification) may not be directly comparable to MHA architectures. This should be discussed.\n- Several appendix subsections reference figures that are described but may not be rendered (e.g., fig_teaser.pdf). Ensure all referenced figures exist.\n","project":"vla-ssd","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260522T021705","overall_score":"weak accept (6/10)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":847,"cache_read_input_tokens":84410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":847,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"d740a259-fa4a-472c-b092-d6ee0e2596fc"}
