{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMLT5zPyMjm42iXmqSN4xD","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Weak Accept (6/10), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Scores:** Quality 6, Clarity 7, Significance 5, Relevance 6, Overall 6.\n\n**Top strengths:**\n- Rigorous 3-arch x 4-suite factorial design with trained/untrained controls\n- Multiple converging analyses (probing, attention, R², RSA, CKA, counterfactual) with strong statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, FDR correction, Bonferroni, TOST equivalence)\n- Honest treatment of metric dependence (RSA vs CKA reversal reported transparently)\n- R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells is a convincing cross-architecture finding\n- Training-vs-inherited decomposition (L1 heads are base-model-dependent) is a genuine methodological contribution\n\n**Top weaknesses:**\n- **No closed-loop behavioral validation** — the ablation result (removing 24/32 instruction heads does not increase MSE) creates an interpretive puzzle about functional importance\n- **Counterfactual \"7-10x amplification\" uses different metrics** for trained (Cohen's d) vs untrained VLM bases (cosine distance); when using the same metric (OXE), trained sensitivity is actually *lower* (d=0.96 vs 1.05)\n- **\"Architecture-independent\" tested only on decoder-only transformers** — cross-attention and diffusion VLAs are untested\n- **Incomplete matrix** (NORA only 2/4 cells; three-tier gradient fully demonstrated only for OpenVLA)\n- **R² exit collapse is descriptive** — no explanation of whether it is beneficial, detrimental, or neutral for task performance\n- **Table 2 claims 6 methods but shows only 2**\n\nThe review report is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T021705/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac7bda5dde6c48a06 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac7bda5dde6c48a06' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 89241\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 158262</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"560a76ea-771e-4c22-8578-c1cddd46925a","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:17:04.129Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nRate on 1-10 scale per dimension. CoRL acceptance rate ~30-40%.\n- **Quality** (1-10): Technical soundness, experimental rigor, claims well-supported\n- **Clarity** (1-10): Well-written, well-organized, reproducible\n- **Significance** (1-10): Results advance the field, practical utility\n- **Relevance** (1-10): Relevance to robot learning\n- **Overall** (1-10): 1-3 reject, 4-5 weak reject, 6 weak accept, 7 accept, 8-9 strong accept\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThis paper presents a systematic token-level analysis framework for understanding how VLA fine-tuning reshapes internal instruction routing. Applied to 3 VLA families (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites (10 of 12 cells populated).\n\n**Three main contributions:**\n1. A token-level analysis framework combining: cross-instruction probing, per-layer R² action probes, attention profiling, RSA, counterfactual perturbation, CKA verification\n2. Two architecture-independent training signatures: (a) R² exit collapse (trained Δ=0.12-0.69 vs untrained Δ=0.01-0.08, 9/10 cells), (b) heightened counterfactual sensitivity (7-10× amplification)\n3. Three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → OXE VLA → task VLA\n\nAdditional observation: RSA inverted-U profile for vision encoder geometry (peak L12), with CKA showing ordering reversal (acknowledged as metric-dependent).\n\n## Key Tables (from paper)\n- Table 1: Cross-instruction probe accuracy at last_preaction (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal, 10-way). Trained: 99.6% at L1, 100% L2+. Untrained (rand): 100% L1, decays to 82% L31.\n- Table 2: Six probing methods on 2 benchmarks, all agree: trained ≈ untrained >> DINO (only 2 of 6 methods shown: temporal ρ and action R²)\n- Table 3: R² exit collapse matrix (3×4). OpenVLA: 0.150/0.130/0.121/0.004(OFT). TracVLA: 0.228/0.138/0.294/0.198. NORA: 0.691/0.302/—/—. Mean=0.226.\n- Table 4: OpenVLA R² exit collapse detail on Goal+Spatial. Trained peak L13-14, exit 0.528-0.540, Δ=0.130-0.150. Untrained (OXE) peak L21-22, exit 0.805-0.812, Δ=0.016-0.020.\n- Table 5: Full 33-layer R² scan on Goal. Trained image_mean peak L8 (0.655), last_preaction peak L13 (0.678). Untrained image_mean peak L0 (0.640), last_preaction peak L32 (0.609). Approx values (~) for exit R².\n- Table 6: Cross-benchmark peak comparison. Goal: L8/L13. Spatial: L13/L21. Shift: +5/+8 layers.\n- Table 7: Counterfactual sensitivity (Cohen's d). Trained Goal: empty 0.96, wrong 0.91, shuffled 0.77, para 0.70. OXE untr Goal: 1.05, 0.84, 0.63, 0.61. Spatial similar.\n- Table 8: Untrained CF responses (cosine distance). Phi-3-Vision Spatial: 0.148/0.078/0.018/0.005. OXE Object: 0.085/0.045/0.052/0.025.\n- Table 9: L1 attention heads. OpenVLA trained 24/32, untrained (rand) 0/32, OXE 25/32. TracVLA trained 27-30/32, untrained 32/32. NORA trained 12-13/16, untrained 13-14/16.\n- Table 10: Three-tier gradient summary. Tier 1 (VLM): no collapse, cos<0.2. Tier 2 (OXE): no collapse, d≈1.05. Tier 3 (task): collapse Δ=0.12-0.69, d=0.7-1.5.\n- Table 11: Three-tier on LIBERO-Goal (OpenVLA). Random init: 0/32 heads, ~0 CF, pathological. OXE: 25/32, d=1.05, Δ=0.020. Task-finetuned: 24/32, d=0.96, Δ=0.150.\n- Tables 12-17: RSA/CKA analysis. SigLIP+Proj RSA=0.615, VLA trained peak 0.488, untrained 0.485, DINO 0.388. Per-layer inverted-U (trained peak L12=0.628). 2×2 training×instruction: RSA invariant in [0.48,0.49]. Cross-encoder: DINOv2>SigLIP>CLIP under RSA. Branch-split: equal branches. RSA vs CKA ordering reversal.\n\n## Experimental Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan includes 17 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- Cross-instruction probing (plan_011, partially plan_005/006/007/008)\n- R² exit collapse matrix (plans openvla_goal_v1, openvla_spatial_v1, d095_synthesis)\n- Counterfactual sensitivity (plans openvla_goal_v1, openvla_spatial_v1, cf_batch_missing_v1)\n- L1 attention analysis (part of standardized pipeline plans)\n- RSA analysis (plans 001, 003, 010)\n- CKA verification (plan_009)\n- Weight analysis (plan_004)\n- Encoder comparison (plans 012, 013)\n- Instruction ablation/invariance (plans 006, 007, 008)\n\nMissing from paper vs plan: Some experiments in the plan (e.g., DINO standalone baseline plan_002 detailed numbers, CLIP control plan_012 full detail, 6-method validation plan_005 showing all 6 methods) are only partially presented — Table 2 shows only 2 of 6 methods.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on methodology and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on methodology and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Detailed Scores** for Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Overall\n6. **Confidence** (1-5 scale: 1=educated guess, 5=certain)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a CoRL venue — robot learning relevance matters. Consider that this is an analysis/understanding paper, not a new method paper.","agentId":"ac7bda5dde6c48a06","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Weak Accept (6/10), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Scores:** Quality 6, Clarity 7, Significance 5, Relevance 6, Overall 6.\n\n**Top strengths:**\n- Rigorous 3-arch x 4-suite factorial design with trained/untrained controls\n- Multiple converging analyses (probing, attention, R², RSA, CKA, counterfactual) with strong statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, FDR correction, Bonferroni, TOST equivalence)\n- Honest treatment of metric dependence (RSA vs CKA reversal reported transparently)\n- R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells is a convincing cross-architecture finding\n- Training-vs-inherited decomposition (L1 heads are base-model-dependent) is a genuine methodological contribution\n\n**Top weaknesses:**\n- **No closed-loop behavioral validation** — the ablation result (removing 24/32 instruction heads does not increase MSE) creates an interpretive puzzle about functional importance\n- **Counterfactual \"7-10x amplification\" uses different metrics** for trained (Cohen's d) vs untrained VLM bases (cosine distance); when using the same metric (OXE), trained sensitivity is actually *lower* (d=0.96 vs 1.05)\n- **\"Architecture-independent\" tested only on decoder-only transformers** — cross-attention and diffusion VLAs are untested\n- **Incomplete matrix** (NORA only 2/4 cells; three-tier gradient fully demonstrated only for OpenVLA)\n- **R² exit collapse is descriptive** — no explanation of whether it is beneficial, detrimental, or neutral for task performance\n- **Table 2 claims 6 methods but shows only 2**\n\nThe review report is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T021705/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":158262,"totalTokens":89241,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3491,"cache_read_input_tokens":85257,"output_tokens":492,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3491},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":85257,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3491,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3491,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"read