{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EevB5kBSxm92BJkR51zAhx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SY28sp8Y17U4qSABpXkMu2","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"vla-ssd","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260522T021705","overall_score":"5 - Weak Reject","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty/Originality and Related Work\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic token-level analysis framework applied to three VLA architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TraceVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites (10 of 12 possible cells). The paper identifies two architecture-independent training signatures—R² exit collapse (9/10 cells) and 7–10× counterfactual sensitivity amplification—and a three-tier routing gradient from VLM base through general VLA pretraining to task-specific fine-tuning. The paper also distinguishes training-induced effects from base-model-dependent effects (L1 head counts).\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Cross-architecture validation is the paper's strongest novelty contribution.** The prior VLA interpretability literature (Grant et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, Swann et al. 2026, Buurmeijer et al. 2026, Molinari 2025, Lu 2025) uniformly analyzes single architectures. Testing three backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL) with different attention mechanisms (MHA vs GQA), parameter scales (3B–7B), and pretraining regimes, and finding convergent signatures, is a genuine advance over the state of the art. The 9/10 cell confirmation of R² exit collapse is convincing evidence of universality rather than artifact.\n\n**S2. The trained-vs-untrained decomposition is novel and well-executed.** No prior work in the VLA interpretability space has systematically compared trained and untrained checkpoints within the same architecture to isolate what fine-tuning creates versus what it inherits. The decomposition revealing that L1 head counts are base-model priors (TracVLA untrained already 32/32) while R² collapse and CF sensitivity are training-induced is a genuinely informative scientific finding. This directly advances beyond Grant et al.'s \"encoded but not used\" behavioral conclusion by providing a representational-level explanation.\n\n**S3. The three-tier routing gradient (VLM → OXE-pretrained → task-finetuned) is a novel conceptual contribution.** Identifying that routing *emerges* at the VLM-to-VLA transition and *reshapes* at the general-to-task transition provides a developmental view of VLA training that is absent from all prior work. The OpenVLA untrained baseline (openvla-7b with 970K OXE episodes, not random init) serves as a natural intermediate that no other paper has exploited.\n\n**S4. The paper is commendably honest about limitations.** The CKA reversal of RSA ordering is disclosed in the main text rather than hidden in an appendix. The OpenVLA Long OFT outlier (1/10 cells failing exit collapse) is reported rather than excluded. The metric-dependence of the inverted-U observation is properly qualified. This transparency strengthens the paper's credibility.\n\n**S5. The related work section (in its current expanded form) is substantially improved.** The paper now cites ~50 references spanning VLA architectures, mechanistic interpretability, behavioral VLA analysis, token-level specialization, and representation similarity methods. The positioning relative to Grant et al. 2026 and Haon et al. 2025 is clear: those works address *what* information VLAs encode; this paper addresses *where* it is redistributed and *whether* the redistribution is architecture-specific. The \"complementary, not competing\" framing is appropriate.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The core methodological contribution is application of established tools to a new domain, not methodological innovation.** Cross-instruction probing, attention profiling, RSA, CKA, R² action probes, and counterfactual perturbation are all well-established techniques in the NLP/vision interpretability literature. The paper's contribution is applying them to VLAs, not developing them. While the *combination* and *factorial design* have value, the individual methods are not novel. For a venue like CoRL, the bar for \"analysis-only\" papers without new methods or closed-loop evaluation is high. The paper should more explicitly argue why the particular combination of tools yields insights that no single tool could provide.\n\n**W2. The three-tier routing gradient, while conceptually appealing, may be partially predictable.** It is perhaps unsurprising that (a) a VLM base without action training shows no action-relevant routing, (b) a model trained on 970K action trajectories develops routing, and (c) task-specific fine-tuning sharpens that routing. The gradient follows from the training progression itself. The paper's contribution here is quantifying the gradient (e.g., CF sensitivity values, R² collapse magnitudes), but the qualitative direction is expected. The paper should discuss whether any aspect of the gradient was *surprising* relative to prior expectations—for instance, that OXE pretraining already creates stronger L1 routing (25/32) than LIBERO fine-tuning (24/32 or 14/32 for OFT), which is genuinely counterintuitive.\n\n**W3. The \"untrained\" baselines are asymmetric across architectures, weakening the cross-architecture claim.** OpenVLA's \"untrained\" is openvla-7b (an OXE-pretrained VLA with 970K trajectories—not a pure VLM), while TracVLA's untrained is Phi-3-Vision (a pure VLM) and NORA's is Qwen2.5-VL-3B (also a pure VLM). The paper acknowledges this in memory.md (\"三架构 untrained 语义不一致\") but the published paper does not sufficiently foreground this asymmetry. The R² exit collapse finding \"trained Δ >> untrained Δ\" is confounded by the fact that the \"untrained\" condition means different things for different architectures. A fairer comparison would require either (a) testing all three architectures with truly random initialization, or (b) testing all three with VLM-only baselines. The current mixed design makes it harder to interpret what \"architecture-independent training effect\" means precisely.\n\n**W4. No closed-loop or behavioral validation.** The paper is entirely open-loop analysis. R² exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are measured on frozen representations without any robot execution. The paper notes \"behavioral_pilot\" experiments are \"准备中\" in report.md, and the paper_framing.md acknowledges \"All analyses are open-loop.\" For CoRL specifically, the absence of any connection to actual robot performance is a significant gap. Even a small-scale demonstration that R² exit collapse magnitude correlates with downstream task performance, or that the three-tier gradient predicts fine-tuning efficiency, would substantially strengthen the paper's practical relevance. Without this, the findings remain descriptive rather than prescriptive.\n\n**W5. The LIBERO benchmark may be too narrow to support \"architecture-independent\" claims.** All experiments use LIBERO (4 suites, 10 tasks each, table-top manipulation). LIBERO has known limitations: single task per layout (Hou et al. 2026), reliance on memorization (Zhou et al. 2025), and limited language grounding demands (Fei et al. 2025). The paper's own literature map catalogs these concerns. The claim of \"architecture-independent routing reorganization\" is tested across architectures but not across benchmarks with fundamentally different task structures (e.g., CALVIN with sequential multi-step goals, Language-Table with continuous language grounding, real-world manipulation). The routing patterns could be LIBERO-specific rather than truly universal.\n\n**W6. The paper does not cite or discuss several directly relevant concurrent works.** The literature_map.yaml lists 9 \"direct competitors\" (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Rizzardo/Buurmeijer 2026, Haon 2025, Molinari 2025, Lu 2025, Fang 2025, Fang CF 2026, Hou 2026), but the related work section in the actual tex file only cites Grant, Haon, Swann, Buurmeijer, Lu, Molinari, Fang, Hou, and Fei—which is good. However, the paper does not discuss Fang et al. 2026 (LIBERO-CF counterfactual benchmark), which is highly relevant given the paper's own counterfactual analysis. Similarly, Zhou et al. 2025 (LIBERO-PRO) and the VLA survey (2025) would strengthen the positioning. The related work in the tex file is much better than the \"7 references\" stated in the task prompt—it actually has ~50 references—but could still benefit from discussing the counterfactual benchmark literature more explicitly.\n\n**W7. The paper's main text still focuses heavily on OpenVLA, with cross-architecture results feeling like a late addition.** The method section describes OpenVLA-7B specifically. The experiments section leads with OpenVLA deep-dive (Sections 4.2–4.3 are all OpenVLA: L1 attention, dose-response, counterfactual). Cross-architecture validation appears as a paragraph within Section 4.2 (\"Cross-architecture generalization\") rather than as a standalone section. Given that cross-architecture universality is the paper's headline claim (title: \"Three Architectures, One Routing Pattern\"), the actual cross-architecture evidence should be more prominent and self-contained. The writing_plan.yaml outlines a better structure (dedicated 4.2–4.5 for cross-arch results) that the current paper does not fully implement.\n\n**W8. Statistical rigor for cross-architecture claims is uneven.** The TOST equivalence test (p < 0.0001) is used for OpenVLA vs TracVLA R² peak comparison, which is good. But the 9/10 cell confirmation of R² exit collapse lacks a formal statistical test—it is presented as a count rather than tested against a null hypothesis. What is the expected base rate of exit collapse under a null model? Without this, \"9/10 cells\" could be less impressive than it appears. Similarly, the \"7–10×\" CF sensitivity amplification is an informal multiplier without confidence intervals.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The \"untrained\" baselines are semantically different across architectures (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLM). Can you provide results with a uniform baseline (e.g., all three architectures tested with their VLM-only checkpoints, excluding OpenVLA's OXE pretraining)? If not, how should we interpret \"architecture-independent training effect\" when the control condition is architecture-dependent?\n\n**Q2.** You report R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells. The one exception is OpenVLA Long (OFT). You attribute this to OFT fine-tuning. Can you elaborate on why OFT specifically prevents exit collapse? Is this an intrinsic property of orthogonal fine-tuning, or could it be a confound of the Long task suite?\n\n**Q3.** The counterfactual sensitivity ordering (empty > wrong > shuffled > paraphrased) is claimed to reflect \"token novelty\" (Spearman ρ = 0.8 with a token overlap metric). But this ordering is also what one would predict from the magnitude of distributional shift in the input. Could you disentangle whether the model is truly sensitive to *instruction content* versus simply responding to *input distribution shift*?\n\n**Q4.** Have you considered testing whether R² exit collapse magnitude predicts downstream task success rate, fine-tuning efficiency, or any behavioral metric? This would transform the finding from descriptive to prescriptive.\n\n**Q5.** The paper notes that TracVLA L1 probe accuracy *decreases* after training (98.1% → 83.6%) despite more instruction-dominant heads (28 → 32), which you call \"information-computation dissociation.\" This is a striking finding that deserves more prominence. Could you elaborate on its implications—does this suggest that VLA training prioritizes action routing over information preservation?\n\n**Q6.** For the three-tier gradient, you have N=1 architecture at the intermediate tier (only OpenVLA has an OXE-pretrained checkpoint). How confident are you that this tier generalizes? Could you test with a second architecture that has a general VLA pretraining stage?\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Originality** | 5/10 | Cross-architecture validation and trained-vs-untrained decomposition are novel contributions to the VLA analysis literature. However, the individual methods are all established, the three-tier gradient is partially predictable, and the paper is fundamentally an application of known interpretability tools to a new domain. The novelty is in the scope and systematic design, not in methodological innovation. |\n| **Clarity** | 7/10 | The paper is well-written with careful statistical reporting (effect sizes, CIs, multiple corrections). The abstract and introduction clearly state claims. The related work is comprehensive. However, the paper's structure is OpenVLA-centric with cross-architecture results feeling bolted on, creating a mismatch between the title's promise and the text's emphasis. |\n| **Significance** | 4/10 | The findings are scientifically interesting but lack demonstrated practical impact. Without closed-loop validation, downstream performance correlation, or actionable design recommendations backed by experiments, the paper's significance for the robot learning community is limited. The \"exit collapse as diagnostic tool\" and \"pretraining-aware fine-tuning\" implications remain speculative. |\n| **Relevance** | 7/10 | Directly relevant to robot learning: understanding VLA internal mechanisms is important for designing better VLA architectures and training procedures. However, the lack of any robot execution experiments reduces the immediate relevance to CoRL's audience, who expect connections to physical systems. |\n| **Overall** | 5/10 | The paper makes a credible scientific contribution through its cross-architecture scope and trained-vs-untrained decomposition, and the writing quality is above average. However, for CoRL main conference, I expect either methodological novelty or demonstrated practical utility—this paper provides neither. The findings are descriptive, the methods are borrowed, and the implications are speculative. The asymmetric untrained baselines and LIBERO-only evaluation further weaken the generality claims. I would recommend this paper for a workshop (e.g., a CoRL workshop on VLA understanding) where the analytical contribution would be well-received, or for a journal venue where the page limit allows fuller cross-architecture treatment. For the main conference, the paper falls slightly below the bar. |\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** — I am confident in my assessment of the novelty landscape and related work coverage. I have read the directly relevant concurrent works (Grant 2026, Haon 2025, Swann 2026) and understand the methodological tools used. My uncertainty is primarily about whether the CoRL 2026 PC would value analysis-only papers more than in prior years, given the growing interest in VLA understanding.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":732,"cache_read_input_tokens":69981,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015aqnmHzHu27rYALNnjZ4kn","session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"fa6d2b07-d71e-42fe-9e98-09869e0a1e17"}
