{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015aqnmHzHu27rYALNnjZ4kn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper makes a genuine scientific contribution through its cross-architecture scope (3 VLA families, 4 LIBERO suites, 10/12 cells) and the trained-vs-untrained decomposition that isolates what fine-tuning creates versus inherits. The R² exit collapse finding (9/10 cells) and the three-tier routing gradient are novel to the VLA analysis literature. However, for CoRL main conference, the paper faces several challenges:\n\n**Main strengths identified:**\n- Cross-architecture validation is the strongest novelty claim (no prior work tests VLA interpretability across multiple backbone families)\n- Trained-vs-untrained decomposition revealing L1 heads are base-model priors, not training effects\n- Honest reporting of limitations (CKA reversal, OFT outlier, metric dependence)\n\n**Main weaknesses identified:**\n- Methods are all established tools applied to a new domain, not methodological innovation\n- No closed-loop or behavioral validation connecting findings to robot performance\n- Asymmetric \"untrained\" baselines across architectures (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLMs) weaken cross-architecture claims\n- LIBERO-only evaluation limits generality claims\n- Paper structure is OpenVLA-centric despite \"Three Architectures\" title\n- Three-tier gradient is partially predictable from the training progression itself\n\n**Recommendation:** Workshop-level contribution for CoRL, or strengthen with behavioral validation and uniform baselines for main conference."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5bf4a7df80157606 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5bf4a7df80157606' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 74929\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 151434</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"a619ca17-f1c5-4e2d-9ea9-da8f4e3935cb","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:17:20.083Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nRate on 1-10 scale per dimension. CoRL acceptance rate ~30-40%.\n- **Originality** (1-10): Novelty of the problem, methods, findings\n- **Clarity** (1-10): Well-written, organized, reproducible\n- **Significance** (1-10): Impact, practical utility, will others build on it?\n- **Relevance** (1-10): Relevance to robot learning\n- **Overall** (1-10): 1-3 reject, 4-5 weak reject, 6 weak accept, 7 accept, 8-9 strong accept\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThis paper presents a systematic token-level analysis framework for understanding how VLA fine-tuning reshapes internal instruction routing. Applied to 3 VLA families (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites (10/12 cells).\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Token-level analysis framework: cross-instruction probing, per-layer R² action probes, attention profiling, RSA, counterfactual perturbation, CKA\n2. Architecture-independent training signatures: (a) R² exit collapse (9/10 cells), (b) 7-10× counterfactual sensitivity amplification\n3. Three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → OXE VLA → task VLA\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper cites 7 references:\n1. Belinkov (2022) - probing classifiers\n2. Brohan et al. (2023) RT-2 - VLA pioneer\n3. Grant et al. (2026) - visual dominance in VLAs\n4. Haon et al. (2025) ICRA - instruction underutilization\n5. Kim et al. (2024) - OpenVLA\n6. Kornblith et al. (2019) - CKA\n7. Kriegeskorte et al. (2008) - RSA\n\n## Key Claims and Evidence\n- **C1 (Framework)**: 6 analysis methods, validated on 3 arch × 4 suites (10 cells). Table 2 shows 6-method validation but only reports 2 methods explicitly.\n- **C2 (Architecture-independent signatures)**: R² exit collapse confirmed in 9/10 cells (Table 3), mean Δ=0.226. CF sensitivity 7-10× (Tables 7-8). L1 attention routing is base-model-dependent, NOT training-induced (Table 9).\n- **C3 (Three-tier gradient)**: VLM→OXE→task (Tables 10-11). Novel finding: routing emerges at VLM→VLA transition, reshapes at general→task transition.\n- **Observation (RSA geometry)**: Inverted-U profile, metric-dependent (RSA vs CKA reversal, Table 17). Explicitly qualified.\n\n## Context for Novelty Assessment\nKey prior works to consider:\n- Grant et al. [3] (2026): Demonstrated visual dominance in VLA action generation (behavioral level)\n- Haon et al. [4] (2025, ICRA): Instruction underutilization across VLA families (behavioral level)\n- Transformer interpretability literature: probing, attention analysis, RSA, CKA are well-established tools\n- VLA architecture papers: RT-2, OpenVLA, etc. focus on performance, not internal mechanisms\n\nThe paper positions itself as bridging the gap between behavioral observations (VLAs underutilize instructions) and mechanistic understanding (how/why at the representational level).\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on novelty and positioning relative to prior work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Detailed Scores** for Originality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Overall\n6. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nKey questions to address:\n- Is the three-tier routing gradient a genuinely novel finding, or a predictable outcome?\n- Is applying existing interpretability tools (probing, RSA, CKA) to VLAs sufficiently novel for CoRL?\n- Are 7 references adequate for a paper making cross-architecture claims?\n- Does the paper sufficiently differentiate from the concurrent Grant et al. (2026) and Haon et al. (2025)?\n- Is this paper more appropriate for a workshop than main conference, given it's purely analysis with no new method or closed-loop evaluation?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Analysis/understanding papers can be valuable at CoRL if they provide actionable insights.","agentId":"a5bf4a7df80157606","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper makes a genuine scientific contribution through its cross-architecture scope (3 VLA families, 4 LIBERO suites, 10/12 cells) and the trained-vs-untrained decomposition that isolates what fine-tuning creates versus inherits. The R² exit collapse finding (9/10 cells) and the three-tier routing gradient are novel to the VLA analysis literature. However, for CoRL main conference, the paper faces several challenges:\n\n**Main strengths identified:**\n- Cross-architecture validation is the strongest novelty claim (no prior work tests VLA interpretability across multiple backbone families)\n- Trained-vs-untrained decomposition revealing L1 heads are base-model priors, not training effects\n- Honest reporting of limitations (CKA reversal, OFT outlier, metric dependence)\n\n**Main weaknesses identified:**\n- Methods are all established tools applied to a new domain, not methodological innovation\n- No closed-loop or behavioral validation connecting findings to robot performance\n- Asymmetric \"untrained\" baselines across architectures (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLMs) weaken cross-architecture claims\n- LIBERO-only evaluation limits generality claims\n- Paper structure is OpenVLA-centric despite \"Three Architectures\" title\n- Three-tier gradient is partially predictable from the training progression itself\n\n**Recommendation:** Workshop-level contribution for CoRL, or strengthen with behavioral validation and uniform baselines for main conference."}],"totalDurationMs":151434,"totalTokens":74929,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3848,"cache_read_input_tokens":70713,"output_tokens":367,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3848},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":367,"cache_read_input_tokens":70713,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3848,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ga7nVLe1QrQ7msKeBrh15x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both reviewers' independent assessments. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\n**Step 1 (Independent)** — Done. R1 gave 6/10 (weak accept), R2 gave 5/10 (weak reject).\n\n**Step 2 (Cross-sharing)** — I need to share each reviewer's findings with the other and have them react.\n\n**Step 3 (Convergence)** — Find common ground and remaining disagreements.\n\n**Step 4 (Synthesis)** — Final consolidated report.\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-share the reviews and get reactions. I'll send messages to both agents simultaneously.\n\n\nActually, I should have the reviewers interact directly with each other rather than me synthesizing this alone. Let me dispatch messages to both agents sharing each other's assessments and asking for their reactions to the other's perspective.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5308,"cache_read_input_tokens":74719,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5308},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"24870b08-db14-480a-a854-6ceb4f7061a9"}
