{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GSaeh7u7f7cfcCBAsXc3Yz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient context. Here is my reaction as R1:\n\n---\n\n**R1 Reaction to R2's Review**\n\nI agree with R2 on several points. The lack of closed-loop behavioral validation is a genuine gap — I flagged this independently. The LIBERO-only scope is a fair limitation, and the OpenVLA-centric paper structure despite cross-architecture claims is a valid editorial critique. I also agree that the asymmetric \"untrained\" baselines (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLMs) is a real methodological concern; our own project notes confirm OXE pretraining already creates strong routing patterns (25/32 instruction-dominant heads), making the trained-vs-untrained comparison semantically inconsistent across architectures.\n\nI partially disagree on two points. First, characterizing this as merely \"established tools applied to a new domain\" undersells the contribution: the trained-vs-inherited decomposition and the convergent exit collapse across three architecturally distinct backbones (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL with GQA) constitute a genuine empirical finding, not just method transfer. Second, the three-tier gradient being \"partially predictable\" does not diminish that it was previously unverified — predictable hypotheses still require empirical confirmation.\n\nR2's review does not change my score. The asymmetric baseline point reinforces my existing concern but was already factored in. I maintain **Overall 6 (Weak Accept)**: a solid empirical contribution with clearly bounded claims, appropriate for a main conference with minor revisions addressing the baseline asymmetry and adding at least a pilot behavioral validation."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abd89c6cd9c72669a (use SendMessage with to: 'abd89c6cd9c72669a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 21594\ntool_uses: 3\nduration_ms: 26071</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"bcde66b2-3c08-4031-b18b-2a849d05e615","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:18:03.364Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in the roundtable discussion phase. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on Methods + Experiments and gave it Overall 6/10 (Weak Accept).\n\nYour scores were: Quality 6, Clarity 7, Significance 5, Relevance 6, Overall 6.\n\nYour key strengths: rigorous factorial design, multiple converging analyses, honest metric dependence reporting, R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells convincing, training-vs-inherited decomposition genuine contribution.\n\nYour key weaknesses: no closed-loop validation, CF \"7-10x\" uses different metrics (Cohen's d vs cosine distance — OXE actually shows d=1.05 > trained d=0.96), \"architecture-independent\" only decoder-only transformers, incomplete matrix (NORA 2/4), exit collapse is descriptive with no functional explanation, Table 2 claims 6 methods but shows only 2.\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 2's review (for your reaction):**\n\nR2 gave Overall 5/10 (Weak Reject). R2's focus was Novelty + Related Work.\n\nR2's key strengths:\n- Cross-architecture validation is genuinely novel (no prior work tests VLA interpretability across multiple backbone families)\n- Trained-vs-untrained decomposition revealing L1 heads are base-model priors\n- Honest reporting of limitations\n\nR2's key weaknesses:\n- Methods are all established tools applied to a new domain, not methodological innovation\n- No closed-loop or behavioral validation\n- Asymmetric \"untrained\" baselines across architectures (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLMs) weaken cross-architecture claims\n- LIBERO-only evaluation limits generality\n- Paper structure is OpenVLA-centric despite cross-architecture title\n- Three-tier gradient is partially predictable from training progression\n\nR2's recommendation: Workshop-level contribution, or strengthen with behavioral validation.\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** React to R2's review in 150-200 words. Address:\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Does R2's review change your score? If so, how?\n4. What is your updated overall assessment?","agentId":"abd89c6cd9c72669a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient context. Here is my reaction as R1:\n\n---\n\n**R1 Reaction to R2's Review**\n\nI agree with R2 on several points. The lack of closed-loop behavioral validation is a genuine gap — I flagged this independently. The LIBERO-only scope is a fair limitation, and the OpenVLA-centric paper structure despite cross-architecture claims is a valid editorial critique. I also agree that the asymmetric \"untrained\" baselines (OXE-pretrained VLA vs pure VLMs) is a real methodological concern; our own project notes confirm OXE pretraining already creates strong routing patterns (25/32 instruction-dominant heads), making the trained-vs-untrained comparison semantically inconsistent across architectures.\n\nI partially disagree on two points. First, characterizing this as merely \"established tools applied to a new domain\" undersells the contribution: the trained-vs-inherited decomposition and the convergent exit collapse across three architecturally distinct backbones (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL with GQA) constitute a genuine empirical finding, not just method transfer. Second, the three-tier gradient being \"partially predictable\" does not diminish that it was previously unverified — predictable hypotheses still require empirical confirmation.\n\nR2's review does not change my score. The asymmetric baseline point reinforces my existing concern but was already factored in. I maintain **Overall 6 (Weak Accept)**: a solid empirical contribution with clearly bounded claims, appropriate for a main conference with minor revisions addressing the baseline asymmetry and adding at least a pilot behavioral validation."}],"totalDurationMs":26071,"totalTokens":21594,"totalToolUseCount":3,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3390,"cache_read_input_tokens":17827,"output_tokens":376,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3390},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":376,"cache_read_input_tokens":17827,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3390,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
