{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xr24dah2oZcDPEjJrjpqKt","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient context to write R2's reaction to R1's review. Here is my response:\n\n---\n\n**R2 Reaction to R1's Review**\n\nI agree with R1 on several points. The ablation puzzle is a serious concern I share — removing 24 instruction heads yielding no MSE increase undermines the paper's functional claims about routing. R1's observation that the \"7-10x\" amplification conflates Cohen's d (trained) with cosine distance (untrained) is sharp and well-taken; when using the same metric on OXE, the gap nearly vanishes (d=0.96 vs 1.05). I also concur that \"architecture-independent\" is overclaimed given all three architectures are decoder-only transformers — no cross-attention or diffusion-based VLAs were tested.\n\nWhere I mildly disagree: R1 rates R² exit collapse as a \"convincing cross-architecture finding\" deserving more credit than I initially gave. Fair — 9/10 cells is robust. But it remains descriptive without mechanistic or behavioral consequence, which R1 also acknowledges.\n\nR1's dissection of the metric inconsistency in the counterfactual claim and Table 2 discrepancy (6 methods claimed, 2 shown) reinforces my original novelty concerns.\n\nR1's review does not change my score. The methodological rigor R1 highlights is real but does not overcome the fundamental issue: established interpretability tools applied to a new domain with no closed-loop validation. I maintain **Overall 5/10 (Weak Reject)**."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aecde10a81a1f992a (use SendMessage with to: 'aecde10a81a1f992a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 25679\ntool_uses: 3\nduration_ms: 23315</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"adef25d8-9468-4b54-a8bd-29719317157b","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:18:06.633Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in the roundtable discussion phase. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on Novelty + Related Work and gave it Overall 5/10 (Weak Reject).\n\nYour scores were: Originality 5, Clarity 7, Significance 5, Relevance 6, Overall 5.\n\nYour key strengths: cross-architecture validation novel, trained-vs-untrained decomposition, honest reporting.\n\nYour key weaknesses: methods are established tools applied to new domain, no closed-loop validation, asymmetric baselines, LIBERO-only, OpenVLA-centric structure, three-tier gradient partially predictable.\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 1's review (for your reaction):**\n\nR1 gave Overall 6/10 (Weak Accept). R1's focus was Methods + Experiments.\n\nR1's key strengths:\n- Rigorous 3-arch × 4-suite factorial design with controls\n- Multiple converging analyses with strong statistics (bootstrap CIs, FDR, Bonferroni, TOST)\n- Honest metric dependence treatment (RSA vs CKA reversal)\n- R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells is convincing cross-architecture finding\n- Training-vs-inherited decomposition is genuine contribution\n\nR1's key weaknesses:\n- **No closed-loop behavioral validation** — ablation result (removing 24 instruction heads doesn't increase MSE) creates interpretive puzzle about functional importance\n- **Counterfactual \"7-10x\" uses different metrics**: Cohen's d (trained) vs cosine distance (VLM untrained). When using same metric on OXE, trained d=0.96 < untrained d=1.05, so the \"amplification\" claim is misleading\n- **\"Architecture-independent\" only tested on decoder-only transformers** — no cross-attention or diffusion VLAs\n- **Incomplete matrix** (NORA 2/4 cells, three-tier gradient only fully for OpenVLA)\n- **R² exit collapse is descriptive** — no explanation of whether beneficial or detrimental\n- **Table 2 claims 6 methods but shows only 2**\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** React to R1's review in 150-200 words. Address:\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Does R1's review change your score? If so, how?\n4. What is your updated overall assessment?","agentId":"aecde10a81a1f992a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient context to write R2's reaction to R1's review. Here is my response:\n\n---\n\n**R2 Reaction to R1's Review**\n\nI agree with R1 on several points. The ablation puzzle is a serious concern I share — removing 24 instruction heads yielding no MSE increase undermines the paper's functional claims about routing. R1's observation that the \"7-10x\" amplification conflates Cohen's d (trained) with cosine distance (untrained) is sharp and well-taken; when using the same metric on OXE, the gap nearly vanishes (d=0.96 vs 1.05). I also concur that \"architecture-independent\" is overclaimed given all three architectures are decoder-only transformers — no cross-attention or diffusion-based VLAs were tested.\n\nWhere I mildly disagree: R1 rates R² exit collapse as a \"convincing cross-architecture finding\" deserving more credit than I initially gave. Fair — 9/10 cells is robust. But it remains descriptive without mechanistic or behavioral consequence, which R1 also acknowledges.\n\nR1's dissection of the metric inconsistency in the counterfactual claim and Table 2 discrepancy (6 methods claimed, 2 shown) reinforces my original novelty concerns.\n\nR1's review does not change my score. The methodological rigor R1 highlights is real but does not overcome the fundamental issue: established interpretability tools applied to a new domain with no closed-loop validation. I maintain **Overall 5/10 (Weak Reject)**."}],"totalDurationMs":23315,"totalTokens":25679,"totalToolUseCount":3,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7267,"cache_read_input_tokens":18047,"output_tokens":364,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7267}