{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YNf4cTmDWSeho5Ss2npcXQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GymdxgGb2pyoaUraFwDzdu","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization in Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Venue:** CoRL 2026  \n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)  \n**Date:** 2026-05-22  \n\n---\n\n## Venue Calibration\n\nCoRL review criteria sourced from CoRL 2023 reviewer guidelines and CoRL 2024 review instructions. Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance. Scale: 1–10 (aligned with CoRL 2023; CoRL 2024 acceptance rate ~39%, historical ~30–40%). Papers evaluated on significance and novelty of results, relevance to robot learning, and potential for scientific/technological impact.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThis paper presents a token-level analysis framework for understanding how VLA fine-tuning reshapes internal instruction routing. Applied to three VLA families (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites (10 of 12 matrix cells populated), the paper identifies two architecture-independent training signatures: (1) R² exit collapse (trained Δ=0.12–0.69 vs untrained Δ=0.01–0.08, confirmed in 9/10 cells), and (2) heightened counterfactual sensitivity (claimed 7–10× amplification). A three-tier routing gradient is proposed: VLM base → OXE VLA → task-finetuned VLA. The paper additionally distinguishes training-induced effects from base-model-dependent effects (L1 attention heads) and reports metric-dependent RSA/CKA findings on vision encoder geometry.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods + Experiments\n\n### Scores\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Quality | 6 |\n| Clarity | 7 |\n| Significance | 5 |\n| Relevance | 6 |\n| **Overall** | **6 (Weak Accept)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 |\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Rigorous factorial design.** The 3-architecture × 4-suite matrix with trained/untrained controls is well-structured. The use of multiple converging analyses (probing, attention, R², RSA, CKA, counterfactual) with appropriate statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, significance testing) lends credibility.\n2. **R² exit collapse is convincing.** 9/10 cells across three architecturally distinct backbones (Llama-2 MHA, Phi-3 MHA, Qwen2.5-VL GQA) is a robust empirical result. The mean Δ=0.226 for trained vs 0.032 for untrained is a clear signal.\n3. **Training-vs-inherited decomposition.** The demonstration that L1 attention routing is base-model-dependent (Table 9) is a genuine contribution that corrects a potential misattribution. The OXE-pretrained OpenVLA comparison (25/32 untrained → 24/32 trained) is particularly convincing.\n4. **Transparent metric reporting.** The RSA vs CKA ordering reversal (Table 17) is honestly reported and framed as enriching rather than undermining the findings. All geometric claims are explicitly qualified by metric.\n5. **OFT outlier handling.** The single exception (OpenVLA Long, Δ=0.004) is well-explained via the parameter-efficient fine-tuning mechanism, with TracVLA Long (Δ=0.198, full fine-tuning, same suite) providing a direct control.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **No closed-loop behavioral validation.** The paper operates entirely in open-loop analysis. The finding that removing 24/32 instruction-dominant heads does not increase MSE (mentioned in the ablation result for the attention heads) creates an interpretive puzzle: if instruction routing is so prominent, why does ablating it have no measurable effect on action quality? This undermines claims about the functional importance of the discovered routing patterns.\n2. **Counterfactual \"7–10× amplification\" is methodologically inconsistent.** Trained models use Cohen's d while untrained VLM bases use cosine distance — these are incommensurable metrics. When the same metric (Cohen's d) is applied to OXE-pretrained models (Tier 2), the trained model actually shows *lower* sensitivity (d=0.96 vs OXE d=1.05 on Goal empty). The \"7–10×\" claim only holds when comparing trained Cohen's d against untrained VLM cosine distance, which is misleading.\n3. **\"Architecture-independent\" is overclaimed.** All three architectures are decoder-only transformers with causal attention. Cross-attention VLAs (e.g., RT-2 style) and diffusion-based action heads are untested. The claim should be scoped to \"decoder-only VLAs.\"\n4. **Incomplete matrix.** NORA covers only 2 of 4 suites. The three-tier routing gradient is fully demonstrated only for OpenVLA (the only architecture with random-init, OXE-pretrained, and task-finetuned variants). TracVLA and NORA lack Tier 2 equivalents.\n5. **R² exit collapse is descriptive, not explanatory.** The paper documents that exit R² drops in trained models but offers no mechanistic explanation (why does training cause this?) and no functional assessment (is this beneficial, neutral, or detrimental to task performance?).\n6. **Table 2 discrepancy.** The paper claims \"six probing methods agree\" but shows only 2 of 6 (temporal ρ and action R²). The remaining 4 methods' results are not presented, making this claim unverifiable.\n7. **Exit R² values in Table 5 are approximate.** Values reported as \"~0.45,\" \"~0.55,\" \"~0.35\" for a paper making quantitative claims about Δ magnitudes is imprecise.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Can you explain the ablation result where removing instruction-dominant heads has no effect on action MSE? Does this suggest the routing patterns you document are epiphenomenal?\n2. Why report CF amplification as \"7–10×\" when this conflates different metrics? What is the amplification when using the same metric?\n3. Can you show the remaining 4 methods from the 6-method validation (Table 2)?\n4. What is the exact (not approximate) exit R² for the full-layer scan (Table 5)?\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Scores\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Originality | 5 |\n| Clarity | 7 |\n| Significance | 5 |\n| Relevance | 6 |\n| **Overall** | **5 (Weak Reject)** |\n| Confidence | 3/5 |\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Cross-architecture scope is the strongest novelty claim.** No prior work has applied systematic interpretability analysis across multiple VLA backbone families. Testing on Llama-2, Phi-3, and Qwen2.5-VL with different attention mechanisms (MHA vs GQA) provides genuine breadth.\n2. **Trained-vs-untrained decomposition is insightful.** Separating what fine-tuning creates (exit collapse, CF sensitivity) from what the base model provides (L1 routing) is a useful conceptual contribution. The finding that training *reduces* instruction-dominant heads in TracVLA (32/32 → 27–30/32) is counterintuitive and valuable.\n3. **Scientific honesty.** The paper consistently qualifies claims: metric dependence is acknowledged, the OFT outlier is not swept under the rug, and the RSA observation is framed as \"qualified.\" This is commendable.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **Methods are established tools applied to a new domain.** Probing classifiers, RSA, CKA, attention analysis, and counterfactual perturbation are all well-established in the transformer interpretability literature. The paper does not propose new analytical methods — it combines existing tools and applies them to VLAs. The \"framework\" contribution (C1) is more of an application recipe than a methodological advance.\n2. **Sparse related work.** Only 7 references is insufficient for a paper making broad claims about VLA internals. Missing citations include:\n   - LLM interpretability work (e.g., Elhage et al., Conmy et al. on circuit discovery)\n   - Multimodal transformer analysis (e.g., work on CLIP internals, VLM representation structure)\n   - Other VLA architectures beyond those tested (RT-2, Octo, π₀)\n   - Probing methodology critiques (beyond Belinkov 2022)\n   - Robot representation learning literature\n3. **Three-tier gradient is partially predictable.** The progression from \"no routing\" (VLM base) to \"general routing\" (OXE pretraining on 970K episodes) to \"task-specific routing\" (fine-tuning) is the expected outcome of progressive training stages. The finding confirms a hypothesis rather than revealing a surprise. The interesting question — *what changes at each transition* — is addressed but only descriptively.\n4. **Asymmetric untrained baselines weaken cross-architecture comparisons.** OpenVLA has three tiers (random init, OXE-pretrained, task-finetuned), but TracVLA and NORA only have two (VLM base, task-finetuned). This means the three-tier gradient is really only validated for one architecture. The claim of \"architecture-independent\" three-tier gradient is underdetermined.\n5. **OpenVLA-centric structure.** Despite claiming three-architecture generality, the paper's detailed analyses (full-layer R² scan, RSA profiles, L1 attention breakdown, three-tier gradient tables) are predominantly OpenVLA results. TracVLA and NORA appear mainly as confirmation in the R² exit collapse matrix (Table 3) and scattered references.\n6. **No actionable design implications.** The paper claims findings \"provide mechanistic grounding for VLA architecture design and training diagnostics\" but offers no concrete recommendations. What should a practitioner do differently based on these findings?\n7. **Relevance gap for CoRL.** CoRL values contributions to robot learning systems. A pure representational analysis paper with no closed-loop evaluation, no new method, and no demonstrated impact on robot performance may be more appropriate for a workshop (e.g., ICML interpretability workshop) or a journal.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. How does this work differentiate from standard transformer interpretability applied to a new model class? What is VLA-specific about the framework beyond the token position decomposition?\n2. Can you demonstrate any actionable design implication? For example, can exit collapse magnitude predict downstream task performance?\n3. Why were only 7 references included? Are there related works in LLM interpretability or multimodal analysis that should be discussed?\n4. Can the three-tier gradient be validated for TracVLA or NORA (do intermediate pretraining checkpoints exist)?\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Discussion\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 reacting to R2:**\n- Agrees on: closed-loop validation gap (independently flagged), LIBERO-only limitation, OpenVLA-centric structure, asymmetric baselines concern.\n- Disagrees on: \"established tools = insufficient novelty\" undersells the contribution — the *findings* (exit collapse across architectures, training-vs-inherited separation) are novel even if the tools are not. Predictable hypotheses still require empirical verification.\n- Score unchanged: 6/10.\n\n**R2 reacting to R1:**\n- Agrees on: ablation puzzle (removing heads with no MSE effect) is serious, CF \"7–10×\" metric inconsistency is sharp and well-taken, \"architecture-independent\" overclaimed for decoder-only only.\n- Acknowledges: R² exit collapse in 9/10 cells deserves more credit than initially given.\n- Disagrees on: methodological rigor does not overcome the fundamental issue of no closed-loop validation for a robotics venue.\n- Score unchanged: 5/10.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Consensus (both reviewers agree):**\n1. ✅ No closed-loop behavioral validation is a significant gap for a CoRL submission\n2. ✅ CF \"7–10× amplification\" claim is methodologically inconsistent (different metrics for trained vs untrained)\n3. ✅ \"Architecture-independent\" should be scoped to \"decoder-only VLAs\"\n4. ✅ Asymmetric untrained baselines weaken cross-architecture claims\n5. ✅ Table 2 shows only 2 of 6 claimed methods — unverifiable\n6. ✅ R² exit collapse (9/10 cells) is empirically robust and the paper's strongest result\n7. ✅ Training-vs-inherited decomposition (L1 heads) is a genuine contribution\n8. ✅ Writing quality and scientific transparency are above average\n9. ✅ Paper is OpenVLA-centric despite cross-architecture framing\n\n**Remaining disagreement:**\n- R1 considers the empirical findings (exit collapse, three-tier gradient) sufficient for main conference with minor revisions; R2 considers established-tools-applied-to-new-domain insufficient novelty for main conference without behavioral validation.\n\n### Step 4: Synthesis\n\n**Consolidated Assessment: Borderline (5.5/10)**\n\nThe paper makes a credible empirical contribution: the R² exit collapse phenomenon (9/10 cells, mean Δ=0.226) is well-documented across three decoder-only VLA architectures, and the training-vs-inherited decomposition of L1 attention routing is insightful. The writing is clear and scientifically honest.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent a confident accept recommendation:\n\n1. **The CF amplification claim (C2b) is partially invalid.** The \"7–10×\" figure conflates incommensurable metrics. When measured consistently (OXE Cohen's d), trained sensitivity is actually *lower* than untrained. This needs correction or reframing.\n\n2. **The three-tier gradient (C3) is only fully validated for one architecture.** Without intermediate pretraining checkpoints for TracVLA and NORA, this claim rests on a single architecture (OpenVLA).\n\n3. **No connection to robot performance.** For a robotics venue, purely representational findings without behavioral consequences leave the \"so what?\" question unanswered. The ablation result (removing instruction heads has no effect) actively undercuts functional relevance.\n\n4. **Incomplete presentation.** Table 2's 6-method claim with only 2 shown, approximate values in Table 5, and NORA's 2/4 coverage weaken the thoroughness narrative.\n\n5. **Sparse related work.** 7 references for a paper spanning interpretability, VLAs, and representational analysis is inadequate.\n\n**What would strengthen the paper:**\n- Pilot closed-loop evaluation correlating exit collapse magnitude with task success rate\n- Consistent CF metrics across all conditions (or honest reframing of the amplification claim)\n- Complete Table 2 (all 6 methods)\n- Expanded related work (15+ references minimum)\n- Scope \"architecture-independent\" to \"decoder-only VLA\"\n\n---\n\n## Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consolidated |\n|-----------|----|----|-------------|\n| Originality | — | 5 | 5 |\n| Quality | 6 | — | 6 |\n| Clarity | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| Significance | 5 | 5 | 5 |\n| Relevance | 6 | 6 | 6 |\n| **Overall** | **6** | **5** | **5.5 (Borderline)** |\n\n**Recommendation:** Borderline. The R² exit collapse finding is the paper's strongest asset and is well-supported. However, the CF metric inconsistency, lack of behavioral validation, incomplete matrix coverage, and sparse related work collectively place this at the boundary. At CoRL's ~35% acceptance rate, this paper would likely fall just below the threshold without revisions addressing the CF claim and adding at least pilot behavioral data.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing paper content against the full experimental plan (review_context.yaml, 17 imagined experiments):\n\n| Experiment | Covered? | Notes |\n|-----------|----------|-------|\n| SigLIP+Proj RSA (plan_001) | ✅ Partial | Table 12, but no bar chart figure |\n| DINO Baseline (plan_002) | ✅ Partial | RSA=0.388 reported, detailed metrics omitted |\n| Per-layer RSA (plan_003) | ✅ Full | Table 13 |\n| Weight Analysis (plan_004) | ✅ Full | Section 4.4.6 |\n| 6-Method Validation (plan_005) | ⚠️ Incomplete | Table 2 shows 2 of 6 methods |\n| Instruction Ablation (plan_006) | ✅ Covered | Via 2×2 matrix (Table 14) |\n| 2×2 Matrix (plan_007) | ✅ Full | Table 14 |\n| Random Instruction (plan_008) | ✅ Partial | Referenced in text, no table |\n| CKA Verification (plan_009) | ✅ Full | Table 17 |\n| LIBERO-Spatial Cross-Benchmark (plan_010) | ✅ Full | Tables 4, 6 |\n| Full-layer R² Scan (plan_011) | ✅ Full | Table 5 (but approximate values) |\n| CLIP Control (plan_012) | ✅ Partial | Table 15, limited detail |\n| Branch-Split RSA (plan_013) | ✅ Full | Table 16 |\n| OpenVLA Goal Pipeline (openvla_goal_v1) | ✅ Full | Tables 4, 5, 7, 11 |\n| OpenVLA Spatial Pipeline (openvla_spatial_v1) | ✅ Full | Tables 4, 6, 7 |\n| Batch Untrained CF (cf_batch_v1) | ✅ Full | Table 8 |\n| D095 Synthesis (d095_synthesis) | ✅ Full | Tables 3, 9, 10 |\n\n**Coverage:** 15/17 experiments covered (some partially). The 6-method validation is the most notable gap — only 2 of 6 methods are shown despite claiming all 6 agree. Overall experiment-to-paper coverage is high (~88%), but selective presentation of the 6-method validation undermines the \"systematic\" narrative.\n","project":"vla-ssd","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260522T021705","overall_score":"borderline (5.5/10)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1334,"cache_read_input_tokens":82531,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1334},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f46fab0-1092-4dec-b5ea-345cef574fce","uuid":"ccf7e442-aacd-4d0d-a31d-8e9c504199de"}
